window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2020-12-1", "source": "2020年12月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part,you are allowed 30 minutes to write on the topic Changes in the Way of Education.You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "Education is undergoing a profound transformation. Not long ago, learning meant sitting in a fixed classroom\nand listening to a teacher; today it is being reshaped by technology into something far more flexible and\npersonal. In my view, these changes make education more open and effective than ever before.\nThe clearest sign of this shift is the rise of online and blended learning. During and after the pandemic, millions\nof students turned to video lectures and learning apps, so that a lesson could be paused, replayed and studied at\none's own pace. Meanwhile, artificial intelligence now recommends exercises based on each learner's weak\npoints, transforming a one-size-fits-all lecture into tailored guidance. These tools break the old limits of time\nand place and let knowledge reach anyone with a phone.\nAdmittedly, no screen can fully replace a caring teacher. Yet the direction is clear: education is moving from\nrigid instruction toward flexible, learner-centred study. If we embrace these tools wisely, learning will become\nboth wider in reach and deeper in quality."}, "translation": {"prompt": "生活在中国不同地区的人们饮食多种多样。北方人主要吃面食，南方人大多吃米饭。在沿海地区，海鲜和淡水水产品在人们饮食中占有相当大的比例，而在其他地区人们的饮食中，肉类和奶制品更为常见。四川、湖南等省份的居民普遍爱吃辛辣食物，而江苏和浙江人更喜欢甜食。然而，因为烹任方式各异，同类食物的味道可能会有所不同。", "sample": "People living in different parts of China have diverse diets. People in the north mainly eat wheat- based foods, while those in the south mostly eat rice. In coastal areas, seafood and freshwater products make up a large proportion of people's diets, while in other regions, meat and dairy products are more common. Residents of provinces such as Sichuan and Hunan generally love spicy food, while people from Jiangsu and Zhejiang prefer sweet food. However, because cooking methods vary, the taste of the same food may differ."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet4/paper/2020-12-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Trust is fundamental to life. If you can't trust anything, life becomes intolerable. You can't have relationships without trust, let alone good ones.\nIn the workplace, too, trust is  __26__  . An organization without trust will be full of fear and  __27__  . If you work for a boss who doesn't trust their employees to do things right, you'll have a  __28__  time. They'll be checking up on you all the time, correcting \"mistakes\" and  __29__  reminding you to do this or that. Colleagues who don't trust one another will need to spend more time  __30__  their backs than doing any useful work.\nOrganizations are always trying to cut costs. Think of all the additional tasks caused by lack of trust. Audit(审计) departments only exist because of it. Companies keep large volumes of  __31__  because they don't trust their suppliers, their contractors or their customers. Probably more than half of all administrative work is only there because of an ever-existing sense that \"you can't trust anyone these days.\" If even a small part of such valueless work could be  __32__  , the savings would run into millions of dollars.\nAll this is extra work we  __33__  onto ourselves because we don't trust people—the checking, following through, doing things ourselves because we don't believe others will do them  __34__  —or at all. If we took all that away, how much extra time would we suddenly find in our life? How much of our work  __35__  would disappear?", "matchIntro": "The Place Where the Poor Once Thrived", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "This is the land of opportunity. If that weren't already implied by the landscape—rolling green hills, palm trees, sun-kissed flowers—then it's evident in the many stories of people who grew up poor in these sleepy neighborhoods and rose to enormous success. People like Tri Tran, who fled Vietnam on a boat in 1986, showed up in San Jose with nothing, made it to MIT, and then founded the food-delivery start-up Munchery, which is valued at $300 million."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Indeed, data suggests that this is one of the best places to grow up poor in America. A child born in the early 1980s into a low-income family in San Jose had a 12.9 percent chance of becoming a high earner as an adult, according to a landmark study released in 2014 by the economist Raj Chetty and his colleagues from Harvard and Berkeley. That number—12.9 percent—may not seem remarkable, but it was: Kids in San Jose whose families fell in the bottom quintile(五分位数) of income nationally had the best shot in the country at reaching the top quintile."}, {"label": "C", "text": "By contrast, just 4.4 percent of poor kids in Charlotte moved up to the top; in Detroit the figure was 5.5 percent. San Jose had social mobility comparable to Denmark's and Canada's and higher than other progressive cities such as Boston and Minneapolis."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The reasons kids in San Jose performed so well might seem obvious. Some of the world's most innovative companies are located here, providing opportunities such as the one seized by a 12-year-old Mountain View resident named Steve Jobs when he called William Hewlett to ask for spare parts and subsequently received a summer job. This is a city of immigrants—38 percent of the city's population today is foreign-born—and immigrants and their children have historically experienced significant upward mobility in America. The city has long had a large foreign-born population (26.5 percent in 1990), leading to broader diversity, which, the Harvard and Berkeley economists say, is a good predictor of mobility."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Indeed, the streets of San Jose seem, in some ways, to embody the best of America. It's possible to drive in a matter of minutes from sleek(光亮的) office towers near the airport where people pitch ideas to investors, to single-family homes with orange trees in their yards, or to a Vietnamese mall. The libraries here offer programs in 17 languages, and there are areas filled with small businesses owned by Vietnamese immigrants, Mexican immigrants, Korean immigrants, and Filipino immigrants, to name a few."}, {"label": "F", "text": "But researchers aren't sure exactly why poor kids in San Jose did so well. The city has a low prevalence of children growing up in single-parent families, and a low level of concentrated poverty, both factors that usually mean a city allows for good intergenerational mobility. But San Jose also performs poorly on some of the measures correlated with good mobility. It is one of the most unequal places out of the 741 that the researchers measured, and it has high degrees of racial and economic segregation(隔离). Its schools underperform based on how much money there is in the area, said Ben Scuderi, a predoctoral fellow at the Equality of Opportunity Project at Harvard, which uses big data to study how to improve economic opportunities for low-income children. \"There's a lot going on here which we don't totally understand,\" he said. \"It's interesting, because it kind of defies our expectations.\""}, {"label": "G", "text": "The Chetty data shows that neighborhoods and places mattered for children born in the San Jose area of the 1980s. Whether the city still allows for upward mobility of poor kids today, though, is up for debate. Some of the indicators such as income inequality, measured by the Equality of Opportunity Project for the year 2000, have only worsened in the past 16 years."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Some San Jose residents say that as inequality has grown in recent years, upward mobility has become much more difficult to achieve. As Silicon Valley has become home to more successful companies, the flood of people to the area has caused housing prices to skyrocket. By most measures, San Jose is no longer a place where low-income, or even middle-income families, can afford to live. Rents in San Jose grew 42.6 percent between 2006 and 2014, which was the largest increase in the country during that time period. The city has a growing homelessness problem, which it tried to address by shutting down \"The Jungle,\" one of the largest homeless encampments(临时住地) in the nation, in 2014. Inequality is extreme. The Human Development Index—a measure of life expectancy, education and per capita(人均的) income—gives East San Jose a score of 4.85 out of 10, while nearby Cupertino, where Apple's headquarters sits, receives a 9.26. San Jose used to have a happy mix of factors—cheap housing, closeness to a rapidly developing industry, tightly-knit immigrant communities—that together opened up the possibility of prosperity for even its poorest residents. But in recent years, housing prices have skyrocketed, the region's rich and poor have segregated, and middle-class jobs have disappeared. Given this, the future for the region's poor doesn't look nearly as bright as it once did."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Leaders in San Jose are determined to make sure that the city regains its status as a place where even poor kids can access the resources to succeed. With Silicon Valley in its backyard, it certainly has the chance to do so. \"I think there is a broad consciousness in the Valley that we can do better than to leave thousands of our neighbors behind through a period of extraordinary success,\" San Jose Mayor Sam Liccardo said."}, {"label": "J", "text": "But in today's America—a land of rising inequality, increasing segregation, and stagnating(不增长的) middle-class wages—can the San Jose region really once again become a place of opportunity?"}, {"label": "K", "text": "The idea that those at the bottom can rise to the top is central to America's ideas about itself. That such mobility has become more difficult in San Jose raises questions about the endurance of that foundational belief. After all, if the one-time land of opportunity can't be fixed, what does that say for the rest of America?"}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Three children in every classroom have a diagnosable mental health condition. Half of these are behavioural disorders, while one third are emotional disorders such as stress, anxiety and depression, which often become outwardly apparent through self-harm. There was an astonishing 52 percent jump in hospital admissions for children and young people who had harmed themselves between 2009 and 2015.\nSchools and teachers have consistently reported the scale of the problem since 2009. Last year, over half of teachers reported that more of their pupils experience mental health problems than in the past. But teachers also consistently report how ill-equipped they feel to meet pupils' mental health needs, and often cite a lack of training, expertise and support from the National Health Service(英国国家医疗服务体系).\nPart of the reason for the increased pressure on schools is that there are now fewer 'early intervention(干预)' and low-level mental health services based in the community. Cuts to local authority budgets since 2010 have resulted in a significant decline of these services, despite strong evidence of their effectiveness in preventing crises further down the line.\nThe only way to break the pressures on both mental health services and schools is to reinvest in early intervention services inside schools.\nThere are strong arguments for why schools are best placed to provide mental health services. Schools see young people more than any other service, which gives them a unique ability to get to hard-to-reach children and young people and build meaningful relationships with them over time. Recent studies have shown that children and young people largely prefer to see a counsellor in school rather than in an outside environment. Young people have reported that for low-level conditions such as stress and anxiety, a clinical setting can sometimes be daunting(令人却步的).\nThere are already examples of innovative schools which combine mental health and wellbeing provision with a strong academic curriculum. This will, though, require a huge cultural shift. Politicians, policymakers, commissioners and school leaders must be brave enough to make the leap towards reimagining schools as providers of health as well as education services.", "47": "Three children in every classroom have a diagnosable mental health condition. Half of these are behavioural disorders, while one third are emotional disorders such as stress, anxiety and depression, which often become outwardly apparent through self-harm. There was an astonishing 52 percent jump in hospital admissions for children and young people who had harmed themselves between 2009 and 2015.\nSchools and teachers have consistently reported the scale of the problem since 2009. Last year, over half of teachers reported that more of their pupils experience mental health problems than in the past. But teachers also consistently report how ill-equipped they feel to meet pupils' mental health needs, and often cite a lack of training, expertise and support from the National Health Service(英国国家医疗服务体系).\nPart of the reason for the increased pressure on schools is that there are now fewer 'early intervention(干预)' and low-level mental health services based in the community. Cuts to local authority budgets since 2010 have resulted in a significant decline of these services, despite strong evidence of their effectiveness in preventing crises further down the line.\nThe only way to break the pressures on both mental health services and schools is to reinvest in early intervention services inside schools.\nThere are strong arguments for why schools are best placed to provide mental health services. Schools see young people more than any other service, which gives them a unique ability to get to hard-to-reach children and young people and build meaningful relationships with them over time. Recent studies have shown that children and young people largely prefer to see a counsellor in school rather than in an outside environment. Young people have reported that for low-level conditions such as stress and anxiety, a clinical setting can sometimes be daunting(令人却步的).\nThere are already examples of innovative schools which combine mental health and wellbeing provision with a strong academic curriculum. This will, though, require a huge cultural shift. Politicians, policymakers, commissioners and school leaders must be brave enough to make the leap towards reimagining schools as providers of health as well as education services.", "48": "Three children in every classroom have a diagnosable mental health condition. Half of these are behavioural disorders, while one third are emotional disorders such as stress, anxiety and depression, which often become outwardly apparent through self-harm. There was an astonishing 52 percent jump in hospital admissions for children and young people who had harmed themselves between 2009 and 2015.\nSchools and teachers have consistently reported the scale of the problem since 2009. Last year, over half of teachers reported that more of their pupils experience mental health problems than in the past. But teachers also consistently report how ill-equipped they feel to meet pupils' mental health needs, and often cite a lack of training, expertise and support from the National Health Service(英国国家医疗服务体系).\nPart of the reason for the increased pressure on schools is that there are now fewer 'early intervention(干预)' and low-level mental health services based in the community. Cuts to local authority budgets since 2010 have resulted in a significant decline of these services, despite strong evidence of their effectiveness in preventing crises further down the line.\nThe only way to break the pressures on both mental health services and schools is to reinvest in early intervention services inside schools.\nThere are strong arguments for why schools are best placed to provide mental health services. Schools see young people more than any other service, which gives them a unique ability to get to hard-to-reach children and young people and build meaningful relationships with them over time. Recent studies have shown that children and young people largely prefer to see a counsellor in school rather than in an outside environment. Young people have reported that for low-level conditions such as stress and anxiety, a clinical setting can sometimes be daunting(令人却步的).\nThere are already examples of innovative schools which combine mental health and wellbeing provision with a strong academic curriculum. This will, though, require a huge cultural shift. Politicians, policymakers, commissioners and school leaders must be brave enough to make the leap towards reimagining schools as providers of health as well as education services.", "49": "Three children in every classroom have a diagnosable mental health condition. Half of these are behavioural disorders, while one third are emotional disorders such as stress, anxiety and depression, which often become outwardly apparent through self-harm. There was an astonishing 52 percent jump in hospital admissions for children and young people who had harmed themselves between 2009 and 2015.\nSchools and teachers have consistently reported the scale of the problem since 2009. Last year, over half of teachers reported that more of their pupils experience mental health problems than in the past. But teachers also consistently report how ill-equipped they feel to meet pupils' mental health needs, and often cite a lack of training, expertise and support from the National Health Service(英国国家医疗服务体系).\nPart of the reason for the increased pressure on schools is that there are now fewer 'early intervention(干预)' and low-level mental health services based in the community. Cuts to local authority budgets since 2010 have resulted in a significant decline of these services, despite strong evidence of their effectiveness in preventing crises further down the line.\nThe only way to break the pressures on both mental health services and schools is to reinvest in early intervention services inside schools.\nThere are strong arguments for why schools are best placed to provide mental health services. Schools see young people more than any other service, which gives them a unique ability to get to hard-to-reach children and young people and build meaningful relationships with them over time. Recent studies have shown that children and young people largely prefer to see a counsellor in school rather than in an outside environment. Young people have reported that for low-level conditions such as stress and anxiety, a clinical setting can sometimes be daunting(令人却步的).\nThere are already examples of innovative schools which combine mental health and wellbeing provision with a strong academic curriculum. This will, though, require a huge cultural shift. Politicians, policymakers, commissioners and school leaders must be brave enough to make the leap towards reimagining schools as providers of health as well as education services.", "50": "Three children in every classroom have a diagnosable mental health condition. Half of these are behavioural disorders, while one third are emotional disorders such as stress, anxiety and depression, which often become outwardly apparent through self-harm. There was an astonishing 52 percent jump in hospital admissions for children and young people who had harmed themselves between 2009 and 2015.\nSchools and teachers have consistently reported the scale of the problem since 2009. Last year, over half of teachers reported that more of their pupils experience mental health problems than in the past. But teachers also consistently report how ill-equipped they feel to meet pupils' mental health needs, and often cite a lack of training, expertise and support from the National Health Service(英国国家医疗服务体系).\nPart of the reason for the increased pressure on schools is that there are now fewer 'early intervention(干预)' and low-level mental health services based in the community. Cuts to local authority budgets since 2010 have resulted in a significant decline of these services, despite strong evidence of their effectiveness in preventing crises further down the line.\nThe only way to break the pressures on both mental health services and schools is to reinvest in early intervention services inside schools.\nThere are strong arguments for why schools are best placed to provide mental health services. Schools see young people more than any other service, which gives them a unique ability to get to hard-to-reach children and young people and build meaningful relationships with them over time. Recent studies have shown that children and young people largely prefer to see a counsellor in school rather than in an outside environment. Young people have reported that for low-level conditions such as stress and anxiety, a clinical setting can sometimes be daunting(令人却步的).\nThere are already examples of innovative schools which combine mental health and wellbeing provision with a strong academic curriculum. This will, though, require a huge cultural shift. Politicians, policymakers, commissioners and school leaders must be brave enough to make the leap towards reimagining schools as providers of health as well as education services.", "51": "Picture this: You're at a movie theater food stand loading up on snacks. You have a choice of a small, medium or large soda. The small is $3.50 and the large is $5.50. It's a tough decision: The small size may not last you through the whole movie, but $5.50 for some sugary drink seems ridiculous. But there's a third option, a medium soda for $5.25. Medium may be the perfect amount of soda for you, but the large is only a quarter more. If you're like most people, you end up buying the large (and taking a bathroom break midshow).\nIf you're wondering who would buy the medium soda, the answer is almost no one. In fact, there's a good chance the marketing department purposely priced the medium soda as a decoy(诱饵), making you more likely to buy the large soda rather than the small.\nI have written about this peculiarity in human nature before with my friend Dan Ariely, who studied this phenomenon extensively after noticing pricing for subscriptions(订阅) to The Economist. The digital subscription was $59, the print subscription was $125, and the print plus digital subscription was also $125. No one in their right mind would buy the print subscription when you could get digital as well for the same price, so why was it even an option? Ariely ran an experiment and found that when only the two \"real\" choices were offered, more people chose the less-expensive digital subscription. But the addition of the bad option made people much more likely to choose the more expensive print plus digital option.\nBrain scientists call this effect \"asymmetric dominance\" and it means that people gravitate toward the choice nearest a clearly inferior option. Marketing professors call it the decoy effect, which is certainly easier to remember. Lucky for consumers, almost no one in the business community understands it.\nThe decoy effect works because of the way our brains assign value when making choices. Value is almost never absolute; rather, we decide an object's value relative to our other choices. If more options are introduced, the value equation changes.", "52": "Picture this: You're at a movie theater food stand loading up on snacks. You have a choice of a small, medium or large soda. The small is $3.50 and the large is $5.50. It's a tough decision: The small size may not last you through the whole movie, but $5.50 for some sugary drink seems ridiculous. But there's a third option, a medium soda for $5.25. Medium may be the perfect amount of soda for you, but the large is only a quarter more. If you're like most people, you end up buying the large (and taking a bathroom break midshow).\nIf you're wondering who would buy the medium soda, the answer is almost no one. In fact, there's a good chance the marketing department purposely priced the medium soda as a decoy(诱饵), making you more likely to buy the large soda rather than the small.\nI have written about this peculiarity in human nature before with my friend Dan Ariely, who studied this phenomenon extensively after noticing pricing for subscriptions(订阅) to The Economist. The digital subscription was $59, the print subscription was $125, and the print plus digital subscription was also $125. No one in their right mind would buy the print subscription when you could get digital as well for the same price, so why was it even an option? Ariely ran an experiment and found that when only the two \"real\" choices were offered, more people chose the less-expensive digital subscription. But the addition of the bad option made people much more likely to choose the more expensive print plus digital option.\nBrain scientists call this effect \"asymmetric dominance\" and it means that people gravitate toward the choice nearest a clearly inferior option. Marketing professors call it the decoy effect, which is certainly easier to remember. Lucky for consumers, almost no one in the business community understands it.\nThe decoy effect works because of the way our brains assign value when making choices. Value is almost never absolute; rather, we decide an object's value relative to our other choices. If more options are introduced, the value equation changes.", "53": "Picture this: You're at a movie theater food stand loading up on snacks. You have a choice of a small, medium or large soda. The small is $3.50 and the large is $5.50. It's a tough decision: The small size may not last you through the whole movie, but $5.50 for some sugary drink seems ridiculous. But there's a third option, a medium soda for $5.25. Medium may be the perfect amount of soda for you, but the large is only a quarter more. If you're like most people, you end up buying the large (and taking a bathroom break midshow).\nIf you're wondering who would buy the medium soda, the answer is almost no one. In fact, there's a good chance the marketing department purposely priced the medium soda as a decoy(诱饵), making you more likely to buy the large soda rather than the small.\nI have written about this peculiarity in human nature before with my friend Dan Ariely, who studied this phenomenon extensively after noticing pricing for subscriptions(订阅) to The Economist. The digital subscription was $59, the print subscription was $125, and the print plus digital subscription was also $125. No one in their right mind would buy the print subscription when you could get digital as well for the same price, so why was it even an option? Ariely ran an experiment and found that when only the two \"real\" choices were offered, more people chose the less-expensive digital subscription. But the addition of the bad option made people much more likely to choose the more expensive print plus digital option.\nBrain scientists call this effect \"asymmetric dominance\" and it means that people gravitate toward the choice nearest a clearly inferior option. Marketing professors call it the decoy effect, which is certainly easier to remember. Lucky for consumers, almost no one in the business community understands it.\nThe decoy effect works because of the way our brains assign value when making choices. Value is almost never absolute; rather, we decide an object's value relative to our other choices. If more options are introduced, the value equation changes.", "54": "Picture this: You're at a movie theater food stand loading up on snacks. You have a choice of a small, medium or large soda. The small is $3.50 and the large is $5.50. It's a tough decision: The small size may not last you through the whole movie, but $5.50 for some sugary drink seems ridiculous. But there's a third option, a medium soda for $5.25. Medium may be the perfect amount of soda for you, but the large is only a quarter more. If you're like most people, you end up buying the large (and taking a bathroom break midshow).\nIf you're wondering who would buy the medium soda, the answer is almost no one. In fact, there's a good chance the marketing department purposely priced the medium soda as a decoy(诱饵), making you more likely to buy the large soda rather than the small.\nI have written about this peculiarity in human nature before with my friend Dan Ariely, who studied this phenomenon extensively after noticing pricing for subscriptions(订阅) to The Economist. The digital subscription was $59, the print subscription was $125, and the print plus digital subscription was also $125. No one in their right mind would buy the print subscription when you could get digital as well for the same price, so why was it even an option? Ariely ran an experiment and found that when only the two \"real\" choices were offered, more people chose the less-expensive digital subscription. But the addition of the bad option made people much more likely to choose the more expensive print plus digital option.\nBrain scientists call this effect \"asymmetric dominance\" and it means that people gravitate toward the choice nearest a clearly inferior option. Marketing professors call it the decoy effect, which is certainly easier to remember. Lucky for consumers, almost no one in the business community understands it.\nThe decoy effect works because of the way our brains assign value when making choices. Value is almost never absolute; rather, we decide an object's value relative to our other choices. If more options are introduced, the value equation changes.", "55": "Picture this: You're at a movie theater food stand loading up on snacks. You have a choice of a small, medium or large soda. The small is $3.50 and the large is $5.50. It's a tough decision: The small size may not last you through the whole movie, but $5.50 for some sugary drink seems ridiculous. But there's a third option, a medium soda for $5.25. Medium may be the perfect amount of soda for you, but the large is only a quarter more. If you're like most people, you end up buying the large (and taking a bathroom break midshow).\nIf you're wondering who would buy the medium soda, the answer is almost no one. In fact, there's a good chance the marketing department purposely priced the medium soda as a decoy(诱饵), making you more likely to buy the large soda rather than the small.\nI have written about this peculiarity in human nature before with my friend Dan Ariely, who studied this phenomenon extensively after noticing pricing for subscriptions(订阅) to The Economist. The digital subscription was $59, the print subscription was $125, and the print plus digital subscription was also $125. No one in their right mind would buy the print subscription when you could get digital as well for the same price, so why was it even an option? Ariely ran an experiment and found that when only the two \"real\" choices were offered, more people chose the less-expensive digital subscription. But the addition of the bad option made people much more likely to choose the more expensive print plus digital option.\nBrain scientists call this effect \"asymmetric dominance\" and it means that people gravitate toward the choice nearest a clearly inferior option. Marketing professors call it the decoy effect, which is certainly easier to remember. Lucky for consumers, almost no one in the business community understands it.\nThe decoy effect works because of the way our brains assign value when making choices. Value is almost never absolute; rather, we decide an object's value relative to our other choices. If more options are introduced, the value equation changes."}, "questions": [{"id": "2020-12-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What is reported in the news?", "options": ["A) Many people have been attacked by Devil Firefish.", "B) The Mediterranean is a natural habitat of Devil Firefish.", "C) Invasive species are driving away certain native species.", "D) A deadly fish has been spotted in the Mediterranean waters."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （主旨题） A deadly fish has been spotted in the Mediterranean waters.\n【定位】A poisonous fish which has a sting strong enough to kill a human is invading the\nMediterranean, warn scientists.\n科学家警告说，一种毒刺强到足以杀死人类的毒鱼正在入侵地中海。\n【信号】新闻首句即导语。答案就在录音第一句 A poisonous fish which has a sting strong\nenough to kill a human is invading the Mediterranean。新闻的开头第一句是\"导语\"，\n按惯例把\"什么东西、在哪、发生了什么\"一次性压缩说完，主旨题的答案几乎必在这里，不\n必等后文。\n【替换】A poisonous fish which has a sting strong enough to kill a human ↔ A deadly fish\n毒到能致命＝deadly\nis invading the Mediterranean ↔ has been spotted in the Mediterranean waters\n正在入侵＝已被发现于该海域\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音只说毒刺\"强到足以杀人\"、且明说 fatalities are rare（致死案例罕见），并没说\"很多\n人已被攻击\"。选项 Many people have been attacked 盗用了录音里 kill a human、\nsting 的危险氛围，把\"有杀人的潜力\"偷换成\"很多人已遭攻击\"，是无中生有的夸大。\n借用原文：Although fatalities are rare, the stings can cause extreme pain and stop people breathing\nB) 与原文相反\n录音明说这鱼 Native to the South Pacific and Indian Ocean（原产南太平洋和印度\n洋），是\"入侵\"地中海，恰恰说明地中海不是它的天然栖息地。选项 The Mediterranean is\na natural habitat 盗用了 the Mediterranean 这个高频地名，却把\"入侵地\"歪曲成\"原产\n地\"，与原文相反。\n借用原文：Native to the South Pacific and Indian Ocean, the potentially deadly fish has\npoisonous hooks\nC) 过度推断\n录音只说环保人士\"担心\"这鱼\"could endanger\"其他海洋生物（还没发生），选项 Invasive\nspecies are driving away certain native species 用现在时把\"可能危及\"说成\"正在赶走本\n地物种\"这一既成事实，且 Invasive species 用复数泛指多种入侵物种，与本则只报道一种\n鱼的范围不符。它盗用了 invasive 这个原词。\n借用原文：environmentalists fear its arrival could endanger other types of marine life", "points": ["听力·长对话", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What is the environmentalist concerned about the spread of Devil Firefish in the Mediterranean?", "options": ["A) It could badly pollute the surrounding waters.", "B) It could pose a threat to other marine species.", "C) It could disrupt the food chains there.", "D) It could add to greenhouse emissions."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It could pose a threat to other marine species.\n【定位】The fish, often known as Devil Firefish, is a highly invasive species, and environmentalists\nfear its arrival could endanger other types of marine life.\n这种鱼通常被称为\"魔鬼蓑鲉\"，是一种高度入侵性的物种，环保人士担心它的到来可能危及\n其他类型的海洋生物。\n【信号】fear＋题干同源词触发定位。答案句里 environmentalists fear 的 fear（担心）与题干的\nconcerned（担心）是同一意思，这个\"担忧\"信号词一响，紧跟其后的 could endanger\nother types of marine life 就是担忧的具体内容——担忧类细节题，答案永远在\"担心\"这\n个词后面那个对象上。\n【替换】could endanger other types of marine life ↔ pose a threat to other marine species\n危及＝构成威胁\nother types of marine life ↔ other marine species\n海洋生物＝海洋物种\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音通篇只说这鱼\"有毒、毒刺能伤人、会危及其他海洋生物\"，从没提它会\"污染水体\"。选\n项 badly pollute the surrounding waters 是考生听到\"poisonous（有毒）\"后顺着常识联\n想出的\"有毒→污染水\"，但原文根本没这句话。\nC) 过度推断\ndisrupt the food chains（扰乱食物链）听上去比\"威胁生物\"更像专业的环保表述，很能唬\n人；但录音对担忧的描述只到 endanger other types of marine life 为止，没有展开到\"食\n物链\"这一层。它是\"听起来更高级、更该说\"造成的诱惑，并非原文内容。\nD) 未提及\nadd to greenhouse emissions（增加温室气体排放）和\"毒鱼威胁海洋生物\"这个话题毫不\n相干，录音从头到尾没提温室气体或气候，是明显的凑数干扰项。\nQuestions 3 and 4 are based on the news report you have just heard.\n1 Almost half the center of Paris will be accessible only by foot or bicycle this Sunday to mark World\nCar-Free Day. This is in response to rising air pollution that made Paris the most polluted city in the\nworld for a brief time. Mayor Anne Hidalgo promoted the first World Car-Free Day last year. Hidalgo\nalso has supported a \"Paris Breathes\" day. On the first Sunday of every month, Paris clears traffic from\neight lanes of the main road. About 400 miles of streets will be closed to cars. It is expected to bring\nsignificant reduction in pollution levels. Last year's car-free day showed a 40 percent drop in pollution\nlevels in some parts of the city, according to an independent air pollution monitor, reports The Guardian\n—and sound levels dropped by 50 percent in the city's center.\n本周日，巴黎市中心近一半的区域将仅允许步行或骑自行车通行，以纪念世界无车日。这是对日益严重的\n空气污染的回应，空气污染曾一度使巴黎成为世界上污染最严重的城市。市长Anne Hidalgo在去年推动\n了首个世界无车日。Hidalgo还支持\"巴黎呼吸\"日。每月的第一个周日,巴黎会清空主干道八条车道的交\n通。约400英里的街道将禁止汽车通行。预计这将显著降低污染水平。据《卫报》报道，根据一家独立空\n气污染监测机构的数据，去年的无车日使该市部分地区的污染水平下降了40%，市中心的噪音水平下降\n了50%。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What will happen on World Car-Free Day in Paris?", "options": ["A) Cars will not be allowed to enter the city.", "B) Pedestrians will have free access to the city.", "C) About half of its city center will be closed to cars.", "D) Buses will be the only vehicles allowed on its streets."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） About half of its city center will be closed to cars.\n【定位】Almost half the center of Paris will be accessible only by foot or bicycle this Sunday to mark\nWorld Car-Free Day.\n本周日，巴黎市中心将近一半的区域只能靠步行或骑自行车进入，以此纪念\"世界无车日\"。\n【信号】新闻首句直接点出当天安排。新闻往往开门见山，第一句 Almost half the center of Paris\nwill be accessible only by foot or bicycle this Sunday 就交代了无车日当天的核心安排\n——\"市中心近一半只能步行或骑车进入\"。这正是\"那天会怎样\"的答案句，信号就是它处在\n新闻开头、与题干\"What will happen\"直接对应。\n【替换】Almost half the center of Paris will be accessible only by foot or bicycle ↔\nAbout half of its city center will be closed to cars.\n正面\"只能步行骑车\"＝反面\"对汽车关闭\"\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 说 Cars will not be allowed to enter the city.，盗用了录音里的 \"Paris\" 和\"汽车不能\n进\"的意思，但把范围从\"近一半市中心\"（Almost half the center of Paris）偷偷放大到\"整\n座城市（the city）\"。录音明明说的是\"half the center\"，A 漏掉了\"half\"和\"center\"这两个\n范围限定，范围被夸大，故错。\n借用原文：Almost half the center of Paris\nB) 未提及\nB 说 Pedestrians will have free access to the city.。录音只说\"只能靠步行或自行车进\n入\"（accessible only by foot or bicycle），意思是\"限定方式\"，根本没提行人\"free（免\n费/免许可）\"。\"free\"是录音里没有的信息，属于无中生有。\nD) 偷换概念\nD 说 Buses will be the only vehicles allowed on its streets.。录音说当天\"只能靠步行或\n自行车\"进入（only by foot or bicycle），allowed 的是 foot/bicycle，根本没提 buses。\nD 借用了\"only…allowed\"的句式结构来唬人，却把允许的对象从\"步行、自行车\"偷换成\n了\"公交车\"，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：accessible only by foot or bicycle", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What motivated the mayor of Paris to promote the first World Car-Free Day in her city?", "options": ["A) The unbearable traffic noise.", "B) The worsening global warming.", "C) The ever-growing cost of petrol.", "D) The rising air pollution in Paris."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） The rising air pollution in Paris.\n【定位】This is in response to rising air pollution that made Paris the most polluted city in the world\nfor a brief time.\n这是为了应对不断加剧的空气污染——这种污染曾一度让巴黎成为世界上污染最严重的\n城市。\n【信号】因果信号 in response to 引出动机。答案句靠 in response to（为了应对……）这个明确\n的因果信号锁定。\"This is in response to rising air pollution\"——\"应对不断加剧的空\n气污染\"正是搞无车日的动机。听到 \"in response to\" 就知道后面紧跟的就是原因，无需\n犹豫。\n【替换】in response to rising air pollution ↔ The rising air pollution in Paris.\n几乎原句搬选项，仅补回地点\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 说 The unbearable traffic noise.。录音确实在结尾提到了声音——\"sound levels\ndropped by 50 percent\"，但那是无车日带来的\"效果\"，而且是\"声音降了\"，不是\"难以忍\n受的噪音\"促使办无车日。A 盗用了录音里关于 sound 的内容，却把\"结果\"错当成\"动机\"，\n答非所问。\n借用原文：sound levels dropped by 50 percent in the city's center\nB) 未提及\nB 说 The worsening global warming.。\"global warming（全球变暖）\"在整段录音里从\n未出现，是凭\"环保话题就该和变暖有关\"的常识硬塞进来的。录音给的动机明确是 air\npollution，不是 global warming。\nC) 未提及\nC 说 The ever-growing cost of petrol.。\"cost of petrol（油价）\"录音里完全没提，同样\n是\"汽车／环保话题常和油价挂钩\"的想当然。录音说的动机是空气污染，与油价无关。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What happened to the Philippine fisherman one day?", "options": ["A) His house was burnt down in a fire.", "B) Many of his possessions were stolen.", "C) His good luck charm sank into the sea.", "D) His fishing boat got wrecked on a rock."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） His house was burnt down in a fire.\n【定位】A Philippine fisherman was feeling down on his luck when a house fire forced him to clear\nout his possessions and change locations.\n一名菲律宾渔夫正觉得自己运气背，这时一场房屋火灾迫使他清空家当、搬离原地。\n【信号】录音开篇第一句即点事件。新闻往往把核心事件放在开篇，这里第一句 a house fire forced\nhim to clear out his possessions 就直接交代了发生的事——一场房屋火灾。新闻题与播\n报同序，第 5 题问“发生了什么”，答案就落在最先念出的这句。\n【替换】a house fire forced him to clear out his possessions ↔ His house was burnt down in a fire\nhouse fire 与 burnt down in a fire 同义\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音里确有 possessions 一词，forced him to clear out his possessions 说的是火灾逼他\n“清理/搬走”家当，不是被偷。选项 Many of his possessions were stolen. 盗用了\npossessions 这个原词，却把“清空”偷换成 stolen（被偷），听到 possessions 就选会中\n招。\n借用原文：clear out his possessions\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音确实提到 good luck charm（护身符）和 ocean/sea，a good luck charm that he\nhad kept under his bed，但护身符是放在床下、并由海里捞出，从未沉入海中。选项 His\ngood luck charm sank into the sea. 拼接了 charm 和 sea 两个原词，编出一个录音没说\n的事件。\n借用原文：a good luck charm that he had kept under his bed\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音里 rock 确实出现，his anchor got stuck on the rock 说的是“船锚卡在岩石上”，因此\n捞起了珍珠，渔船并没有触礁损毁。选项 His fishing boat got wrecked on a rock. 盗用了\nrock 这个原词，把“锚卡石头”夸大成“船触礁失事”，与录音不符。\n借用原文：his anchor got stuck on the rock", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What was the fisherman forced to do?", "options": ["A) Change his fishing locations.", "B) Find a job in a travel agency.", "C) Sell the pearl he had kept for years.", "D) Spend a few nights on a small island."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Sell the pearl he had kept for years.\n【定位】When he was forced to sell it, the shocked tourist agent at Puerto Princesa told him that the\n77-pound giant pearl ... was the biggest pearl in the world.\n当他被迫卖掉这颗珍珠时，普林塞萨港那位震惊的旅游代理告诉他，这颗 77 磅的巨型珍珠\n是世界上最大的珍珠。\n【信号】题干强制语 forced 在原文原样出现。题干问 forced to do，录音里出现 When he was\nforced to sell it——forced 这个强制语在原文照样念出，它后面紧跟的动词 sell 就是答案\n动作。强制语原词重现是最直接的定位信号。\n【替换】When he was forced to sell it ↔ Sell the pearl he had kept for years\nsell it 与 Sell the pearl 同指\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音里 change locations 确实念到，forced him to clear out his possessions and\nchange locations 指火灾后“搬家/换住处”，不是换打鱼的地方。选项 Change his fishing\nlocations. 盗用 change locations 并擅自加上 fishing，把“搬家”偷换成“换渔场”。\n借用原文：change locations\nB) 答非所问\n录音提到 tourist agent（旅游代理人），the shocked tourist agent at Puerto\nPrincesa，选项 Find a job in a travel agency. 借这个相近词造出“去旅行社找工作”，但录\n音从没说渔夫去旅行社上班，是答非所问。\n借用原文：tourist agent at Puerto Princesa\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音确有 island，while sailing off the coastal island in the Philippines 是说他在海边小\n岛附近航行时捞到珍珠，并非被迫去岛上住几晚。选项 Spend a few nights on a small\nisland. 盗用 island 这个原词编出一个录音没有的动作。\n借用原文：sailing off the coastal island in the Philippines", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What did the fisherman learn from the tourist agent?", "options": ["A) His pearl could be displayed in a museum.", "B) His monstrous pearl was extremely valuable.", "C) The largest pearl in the world weighs 14 pounds.", "D) A New York museum has the world's biggest pearl."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） His monstrous pearl was extremely valuable.\n【定位】the shocked tourist agent ... told him that the 77-pound giant pearl that he'd kept hidden\nin his run-down wooden house was the biggest pearl in the world, which was valued at 76\nmillion pounds.\n那位震惊的旅游代理告诉他，他藏在破旧木屋里的这颗 77 磅巨型珍珠是世界上最大的珍\n珠，价值 7600 万英镑。\n【信号】告知动词 told him that 后接答案。题干问“从代理那里得知什么”，录音里 told him that\nthe 77-pound giant pearl ... was the biggest pearl in the world, which was valued at\n76 million pounds——告知动词 told him that 后面的整句就是他获知的信息，世界最\n大、价值 7600 万英镑都在此。\n【替换】was the biggest pearl in the world, which was valued at 76 million pounds ↔\nHis monstrous pearl was extremely valuable\n最大/值 7600 万 概括为 extremely valuable\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n录音末尾确有 going on display 与 museum，before hopefully going on display to\nattract more tourists，但这是记者对珍珠未来去向的补充，不是 tourist agent 告诉渔夫\n的内容。题问“从代理处得知什么”，A 盗用了 display 原词却把出处张冠李戴。\n借用原文：going on display to attract more tourists\nC) 与原文相反\n录音里 14 pounds 确实念到，The pearl of Allah, which is currently on display in a\nNew York museum only weighs 14 pounds，但 14 磅是纽约馆那颗“真主之珠”的重量，\n且它比渔夫这颗小 5 倍，绝非“世界最大”。选项 The largest pearl in the world weighs 14\npounds. 把 14 磅安到“世界最大珍珠”头上，与原文相反。\n借用原文：only weighs 14 pounds. That is 5 times smaller than the pearl that the fisherman just\nhanded in\nD) 与原文相反\n录音提到 New York museum，The pearl of Allah, which is currently on display in a\nNew York museum，但纽约馆藏的“真主之珠”比渔夫这颗小 5 倍，世界最大的是渔夫这\n颗。选项 A New York museum has the world's biggest pearl. 盗用 New York museum\n原词，却把“世界最大”错按在它头上，与原文相反。\n借用原文：The pearl of Allah, which is currently on display in a New York museum only weighs\n14 pounds", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What do we learn about the woman's company?", "options": ["A) It boasts a fairly long history.", "B) It has over 50 business partners.", "C) It has 75 offices around the world.", "D) It produces construction materials."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It boasts a fairly long history.\n【定位】Astra Consultants has branches in over 50 countries, offering different business services. We\nare a global company with 75 years of history, and our clients include some of the world's\nlargest companies.\nAstra 咨询公司在 50 多个国家设有分支机构，提供各类商务服务。我们是一家拥有 75 年历\n史的全球性公司，客户中不乏世界上一些最大的企业。\n【信号】女士自述公司即被介绍方的应答。这是女士在介绍『我们公司』的应答段，凡她报出的数字\n与年限都是答案候选。关键句 We are a global company with 75 years of history 把\n『75 年历史』直接念出，对应『历史悠久』这一条。\n【替换】with 75 years of history ↔ It boasts a fairly long history.\n具体年限概括成『历史悠久』\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音说的是 branches in over 50 countries——50 多个『国家』有分支，B 却说 It has\nover 50 business partners.『50 多个商业伙伴』。它偷了原文的数字 50，却把 countries\n偷换成 partners，是听到数字就想选的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：branches in over 50 countries\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 说 It has 75 offices around the world.『全球 75 个办公室』，数字 75 确在录音里，但\n原文是 75 years of history——75 修饰的是『年』不是『办公室』。它把 years 偷换成\noffices，又是典型原词陷阱。\n借用原文：75 years of history\nD) 张冠李戴\nD 说 It produces construction materials.『生产建筑材料』，但生产建筑用石材的是男士\n的家族企业 We manufacture and export stone for buildings，女士公司是做咨询的。这\n是把男方公司的业务安到女方公司头上的张冠李戴。\n借用原文：We manufacture and export stone for buildings and other constructions", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What does the man say about his own company?", "options": ["A) It was started by his father.", "B) It has about 50 employees.", "C) It is over 100 years old.", "D) It is a family business."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It is a family business.\n【定位】This is a family business started by my grandfather in 1950. We employ just over 100 people.\n这是一家由我祖父在 1950 年创办的家族企业。我们雇用的员工刚刚超过 100 人。\n【信号】男方应答中亲口定性『家族企业』。这是男士回应女士『介绍一下你公司』的应答句，他亲\n口说 This is a family business，直接给出公司性质——被问者的应答里直陈的定性就是答\n案落点。\n【替换】This is a family business ↔ It is a family business.\n公司性质几乎原义对应\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 说 It was started by his father.『父亲创办』，录音确有『谁创办』这一信息，但原文是\nstarted by my grandfather——是『祖父』。它偷换了亲属辈分，是听到『started by』\n就想选的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：started by my grandfather in 1950\nB) 原词陷阱\nB 说 It has about 50 employees.『约 50 名员工』，录音是 We employ just over 100\npeople『100 多人』。它借用了『员工人数』这个点，却把数字从 100 改成 50，是数字型\n原词陷阱。\n借用原文：We employ just over 100 people\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 说 It is over 100 years old.『有 100 多年历史』，录音里 100 确实出现，但 just over\n100 people 的 100 修饰的是『人』。原文还说创办于 in 1950，到今不足百年。它把\n『100 个人』偷换成『100 年』，是数字张冠李戴的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：We employ just over 100 people", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What is the main problem with the man's company?", "options": ["A) Outdated product design.", "B) Loss of competitive edge.", "C) Shortage of raw material supply.", "D) Legal disputes in many countries."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Loss of competitive edge.\n【定位】Our main challenge is that our national currency is rising, and we're losing competitive\nadvantage to stone producers in India.\n我们的主要挑战是本国货币正在升值，我们正在对印度的石材生产商失去竞争优势。\n【信号】challenge 一词直接引出困难句。男方用 Our main challenge is that 这个标志短语亲口宣\n布主要困难，其后半句 we're losing competitive advantage 就是答案落点——『main\nchallenge』几乎等同于题干的『main problem』，信号极明确。\n【替换】we're losing competitive advantage to stone producers in India ↔ Loss of competitive edge.\n动词短语名词化、advantage=edge\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 说 Outdated product design.『产品设计过时』。男方讲的是客户要 a special kind of\nstone cut in a special design，只提到『按特殊设计切割石材』，从没说设计『过时』。\n这是借 design 一词、却把『特殊设计』曲解为『过时设计』的诱导。\n借用原文：a special kind of stone cut in a special design\nC) 未提及\nC 说 Shortage of raw material supply.『原料供应短缺』。男方明说主要挑战是 our\nnational currency is rising『货币升值』导致竞争力下降，全程没提原料短缺。这是把真实\n困难偷换成另一种听上去合理的麻烦。\nD) 原词陷阱\nD 说 Legal disputes in many countries.『多国法律纠纷』。录音确实出现了印度和『国\n家』，但 stone producers in India 说的是印度的竞争对手，不是法律纠纷；女士提的\nlegal issues 也只是顾问可服务范围，与男方困难无关。它借了原文的地点与法律词来设\n套。\n借用原文：losing competitive advantage to stone producers in India", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What does the woman suggest doing to help the man's company?", "options": ["A) Introducing innovative marketing strategies.", "B) Seeking new ways to increase its exports.", "C) Providing training for its staff members.", "D) Conducting a financial analysis for it."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Conducting a financial analysis for it.\n【定位】I would suggest that you let us first conduct a financial analysis of your company, together\nwith an analysis of your competitors in India.\n我建议你先让我们对贵公司做一次财务分析，同时分析一下你在印度的竞争对手。\n【信号】I would suggest 直接引出建议。女士用 I would suggest that 这一建议标志句开口，紧\n跟的动作 conduct a financial analysis 就是她建议先做的事；suggest 与题干 suggest\ndoing 严丝合缝，信号最明确。\n【替换】conduct a financial analysis of your company ↔ Conducting a financial analysis for it.\n动词原形改成动名词、措辞几近一致\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 说 Introducing innovative marketing strategies.『引入创新营销策略』。女士只笼统\n说能在 from market analysis to legal issues『从市场分析到法律问题』各方面提供建议，\n但具体建议的第一步是财务分析，从未提『营销策略』。这是借 market 一词诱导的未提及\n项。\n借用原文：from market analysis to legal issues\nB) 原词陷阱\nB 说 Seeking new ways to increase its exports.『想办法增加出口』。录音里 export 确\n实出现，但来自男方介绍业务 We manufacture and export stone，女士的建议是做财务\n分析，没说去增加出口。它盗用 export 一词，把男方的业务说成女方的建议。\n借用原文：We manufacture and export stone for buildings and other constructions\nC) 未提及\nC 说 Providing training for its staff members.『为员工提供培训』。录音里既无 training\n也无 staff，女士的建议只有财务分析与竞争对手分析。这是录音根本没出现过的内容。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What do we learn about the woman from the conversation?", "options": ["A) She is a real expert at house decorations.", "B) She is really impressed by the man's house.", "C) She is well informed about the design business.", "D) She is attracted by the color of the sitting room."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （推断题） She is really impressed by the man's house.\n【定位】W: I honestly am impressed. I know I could probably repaint the walls in my house over a\nweekend or something, but not a full renovation.\n女：说真的，我太佩服了。我知道自己周末大概能把家里的墙重新刷一遍之类，但做不了整\n套翻新。\n【信号】本人直陈感受+转折露底。信号有两层：一是她直接说 I honestly am impressed（亲口表\n态被打动）；二是转折 but not a full renovation，承认自己最多刷刷墙、做不了整套翻新\n——转折后才是她的真实水平。两处合起来锁定她是「被打动的外行」，而非行家。\n【替换】I honestly am impressed ↔ She is really impressed by the man's house.\n原话补出对象\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\na real expert at house decorations 与事实相反：她明说 not a full renovation、I\nwouldn't know where to start，自认不会整套翻新，根本不是装修专家。decorations 一\n词贴着话题，听到容易顺手选。\n借用原文：I wouldn't know where to start\nC) 原词陷阱\nwell informed about the design business 偷用了对话里的话题词 design——但谈\ndesign 的是男士的房子像请了 professional interior designer，并非说「她」懂设计行\n业。她从未表现出对设计行业内行，反而说不知从何下手。\n借用原文：professional interior designer\nD) 未提及\nattracted by the color of the sitting room 里「客厅 sitting room」确实在末段她自己家\n那句出现过（renovating our sitting room），但那是说她想翻新自家客厅，录音从没说她\n被「男士客厅的颜色」吸引，color 更是原文没提的。\n借用原文：renovating our sitting room", "points": ["听力·篇章", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "Where did the man get his ideas for the project?", "options": ["A) From a construction businessman.", "B) From his younger brother Greg.", "C) From home design magazines.", "D) From a professional interior designer."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） From home design magazines.\n【定位】M: Well, for a while now, I've been regularly buying home design magazines. Every now and\nthen, I'd save a picture I liked.\n男：嗯，有一阵子了，我一直定期买家居设计杂志。时不时地，我会把喜欢的图片存下来。\n【信号】被问者对Where的直接应答。女方刚问 Where did you get your ideas?，男方的应答 I've\nbeen regularly buying home design magazines 正是对这个 Where 的直接回答——对\n话题里，答案就落在被问者紧接着的回应中。\n【替换】buying home design magazines ↔ From home design magazines.\n近乎同词的来源\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\na construction businessman 偷用原文 He's actually in the construction business——\nGreg 确实搞建筑，但那是帮忙施工 / 拿到折扣材料，不是点子的来源。把「帮忙的人」当\n成「点子来源」正是出题陷阱。\n借用原文：He's actually in the construction business\nB) 张冠李戴\nhis younger brother Greg 用了原文人名 Greg（a little help from my brother Greg），\n但 Greg 是帮他施工、弄到折扣材料的人，点子是他自己从杂志攒来的。听到人名就选是典\n型失分点。\n借用原文：a little help from my brother Greg\nD) 与原文相反\na professional interior designer 偷用原文 professional interior designer——但那句是\n女方猜「看起来像请了专业设计师吧？」，男方紧接着否认 No. I did it all by myself。原\n文恰恰说没请设计师，选项却拿它当来源。\n借用原文：No. I did it all by myself", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What did the man say about the project he recently completed?", "options": ["A) The cost was affordable.", "B) The style was fashionable.", "C) The effort was worthwhile.", "D) The effect was unexpected."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The cost was affordable.\n【定位】M: Actually it was surprisingly affordable. I managed to sell a lot of my old furniture and put\nthat extra money towards the new material.\n男：其实它便宜得出乎意料。我设法卖掉了不少旧家具，把那笔额外的钱投到新材料上。\n【信号】对贵不贵之问的直接应答。女方先问 Was it very expensive?，男方应答 Actually it was\nsurprisingly affordable，正面回答了「花费」这一维——被问者对「贵不贵」的直接表\n态，就是答案句。\n【替换】surprisingly affordable ↔ The cost was affordable.\n同词换说花费\nWas it very expensive ↔ The cost was affordable.\n问贵被否再正向作答\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nThe style was fashionable. 里 style 偷用了原文 my overall style was quite minimal\n——但他说自己风格是「极简 minimal」，从没说「时髦 fashionable」，且这题问的是他\n对工程的总评，原文正面谈的是花费不是风格。\n借用原文：my overall style was quite minimal\nC) 未提及\nThe effort was worthwhile. 说付出值得，但录音里他谈的是花费便宜、卖旧家具凑钱，从\n没正面说「这份付出值不值」。worthwhile 是原文没给出的评价。\nD) 偷换概念\nThe effect was unexpected. 说效果出乎意料。原文 surprisingly 修饰的是「affordable\n（便宜得意外）」，而不是「效果」；把 surprisingly 错接到「effect」上，是偷换对象。\n借用原文：surprisingly affordable", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "Why does the woman invite the man to her house next week?", "options": ["A) She'd like him to talk with Jonathan about a new project.", "B) She'd like to show him around her newly-renovated house.", "C) She wants to discuss the house decoration budget with him.", "D) She wants him to share his renovation experience with her."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （目的题） She wants him to share his renovation experience with her.\n【定位】W: Jonathan and I are thinking of renovating our sitting room... And we'd love to get some\ninspiration from your experience. Are you free to come over for a coffee early next week?\n女：我和 Jonathan 正考虑翻新我们的客厅……我们很想从你的经验里取点灵感。你下周早\n些时候有空过来喝杯咖啡吗？\n【信号】邀请前的I'd love to表目的。请求句 Are you free to come over 之前那句 we'd love to\nget some inspiration from your experience 正点明邀请缘由——「I'd love to + 想得到\n什么」是目的题的落点信号。\n【替换】get some inspiration from your experience ↔\nShe wants him to share his renovation experience with her.\n取经=请其分享经验\npick your brains a bit more ↔ share his renovation experience with her\n习语pick your brains=取经\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\ntalk with Jonathan about a new project 偷用原文人名 Jonathan（Jonathan and I are\nthinking of renovating）——但 Jonathan 是和她一起想翻新客厅的人，她从没说请男士\n去「和 Jonathan 谈一个新项目」。听到 Jonathan 就选是陷阱。\n借用原文：Jonathan and I are thinking of renovating\nB) 与原文相反\nshow him around her newly-renovated house 说带他参观她「已翻新」的家——但她明\n说 thinking of renovating our sitting room, not the whole house, not yet anyway，是\n「正打算翻、还没翻」。把她还没做的事说成已完成，是时态 / 事实陷阱。\n借用原文：not the whole house, not yet anyway\nC) 未提及\ndiscuss the house decoration budget 里 budget 是钱的话题，蹭了前文谈花费\n（affordable）的印象——但她请他来是为取经，全程没提要和他谈「预算」。这是把别处\n的话题硬搬过来。", "points": ["听力·篇章", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What does England spend an annual three million pounds on?", "options": ["A) Paying hospital bills for emergency cases.", "B) Doing research on ear, nose and throat diseases.", "C) Removing objects from patients' noses and ears.", "D) Providing routine care for small children."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Removing objects from patients' noses and ears.\n【定位】Removing foreign objects from ears and noses costs England almost three million pounds a\nyear, a study suggests.\n一项研究显示，从耳朵和鼻子里取出异物每年要花费英国近三百万英镑。\n【信号】花费数字句——costs…three million pounds。答案句的信号是\"动作 + 花费 + 三百\n万\"这一结构：Removing foreign objects from ears and noses costs England almost\nthree million pounds a year 直接把题干里的 three million pounds 和它的用途（取异\n物）绑在一句，听到 costs…three million pounds 就锁定这是答案句。\n【替换】Removing foreign objects from ears and noses ↔\nRemoving objects from patients' noses and ears.\nforeign objects=objects、词序调换的同义改写\n【排除】A) 答非所问\n录音通篇在讲医院取异物，孩子会联想到\"付医院账单\"，但原文从未说这三百万是用来\nPaying hospital bills for emergency cases.，只说花在\"取异物\"这个动作上，付账单是\n凭\"医院\"二字脑补出来的。\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音后文确实出现 Any ear, nose and throat surgeon，选项 Doing research on ear,\nnose and throat diseases. 原样搬来 ear, nose and throat 这串词，但那是在讲外科医生\n的奇闻，不是说三百万花在\"做研究\"上，听到熟词就选会中招。\n借用原文：Any ear, nose and throat surgeon has many weird stories\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音反复提到 children，选项 Providing routine care for small children. 借这个高频词编\n出\"日常护理\"，但原文说钱花在\"取异物\"这个具体动作上，从没说花在\"日常护理\"，属拿熟\n词编新用途。\n借用原文：Children were responsible for the vast majority of cases", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What do we learn from England's Hospital Episode Statistics?", "options": ["A) Children aged one to four are often more curious than older children.", "B) Five- to nine-year-olds are the most likely to put things in their ears.", "C) Many children like to put foreign objects in their mouths.", "D) Many children like to smell things they find or play with."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Five- to nine-year-olds are the most likely to put things in their ears.\n【定位】According to England's Hospital Episode Statistics, children aged one to four were the most\nlikely to need help from doctors for a foreign object in their nose. Five- to nine-year-olds\ncome to the hospital with something in their ears the most.\n根据英国医院病例统计，1 到 4 岁的儿童最有可能因为鼻子里有异物而需要医生帮助。5 到\n9 岁的儿童最常因为耳朵里有东西而到医院就诊。\n【信号】专有名词 + 两个年龄段并列。信号是题干专有名词的原样复现 According to England's\nHospital Episode Statistics，紧接其后是两句年龄段并列陈述，把\"1-4 岁→鼻\"\"5-9 岁→\n耳\"分别说清，正是要核对的答案句。\n【替换】Five- to nine-year-olds come to the hospital with something in their ears the most ↔\nFive- to nine-year-olds are the most likely to put things in their ears.\ncome to hospital with…the most=most likely to put…，结果换说成行为\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确有 children aged one to four were the most likely to need help…for a foreign\nobject in their nose，但说的是\"1-4 岁→鼻子\"，选项 Children aged one to four are\noften more curious than older children. 盗用了 one to four 这个年龄段，却把后半改成\n原文没说的\"比大孩子更好奇\"，年龄对、结论错。\n借用原文：children aged one to four were the most likely to need help from doctors for a foreign\nobject in their nose\nC) 偷换概念\n录音只讲鼻（nose）和耳（ears），选项 Many children like to put foreign objects in\ntheir mouths. 借 foreign objects 这个原词，却把部位换成了\"嘴 mouths\"，原文从未说孩\n子往嘴里塞东西。\n借用原文：Removing foreign objects from ears and noses\nD) 未提及\n选项 Many children like to smell things they find or play with. 说孩子\"喜欢闻东西\"，但\n这份统计句里只讲塞鼻塞耳、就医取物，全篇都没提\"smell（闻）\"这个行为，属凭\"鼻子\"联\n想脑补。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What is generally believed to account for children putting things in their ears or noses?", "options": ["A) They want to attract attention.", "B) They tend to act out of impulse.", "C) They are unaware of the potential risks.", "D) They are curious about these body parts."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They are curious about these body parts.\n【定位】According to the study, the occurrence of foreign objects in children is generally attributed to\ncuriosity. Children have an impulse to explore their noses and ears.\n根据这项研究，儿童体内出现异物的情况通常被归因于好奇心。孩子有一种探索自己鼻子和\n耳朵的冲动。\n【信号】因果信号 attributed to 后面的 curiosity。答案信号是因果短语 is generally attributed\nto curiosity——attributed to（归因于）正是\"导致原因\"的信号，它后面紧跟的 curiosity\n就是公认原因，听到 attributed to 立刻锁定其后那个词。\n【替换】is generally attributed to curiosity ↔ They are curious about these body parts.\n名词 curiosity ↔ 形容词 curious，名词转形容词\nChildren have an impulse to explore their noses and ears ↔ curious about these body parts\n\"想探索鼻耳\"=\"对这些身体部位好奇\"，详述换概括\n【排除】A) 未提及\n选项 They want to attract attention. 把原因说成\"想引起注意\"，但录音归因句明说原因是\ncuriosity，全篇没提 attract attention，属另起一个原文没有的原因。\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音确有 Children have an impulse to explore their noses and ears，选项 They tend\nto act out of impulse. 原样搬来 impulse 这个词，但原文的\"冲动\"是\"探索鼻耳的冲动\"、\n用来佐证好奇，并非把\"一时冲动\"本身当作公认原因，题目问的公认原因是 curiosity。\n借用原文：Children have an impulse to explore their noses and ears\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音后文提到 Batteries can pose a particular danger，让\"没意识到风险\"显得合理，但选\n项 They are unaware of the potential risks. 说的\"没意识到危险\"原文从未当作孩子塞东西\n的原因，公认原因是好奇，这是把后文的\"危险\"错接成原因。\n借用原文：Batteries can pose a particular danger", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What did ReBicycle do to help Leila Rahimi?", "options": ["A) It gave her a used bicycle.", "B) It paid for her English lessons.", "C) It delivered her daily necessities.", "D) It provided her with physical therapy."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It gave her a used bicycle.\n【定位】At this time, ReBicycle got involved and gave Leila a second-hand bicycle.\n就在这时，ReBicycle 介入进来，给了 Leila 一辆二手自行车。\n【信号】人名出场后紧跟的动作句。题干点的人名 Leila 在原文反复出现，而真正承载「为她做了什\n么」的，是 ReBicycle got involved and gave Leila a second-hand bicycle 这句——机\n构名 ReBicycle 与人名 Leila 同时出现、又带了 gave 这个给予动作，正是答案句的信号。\n【替换】a second-hand bicycle ↔ a used bicycle\nsecond-hand = used 同义替换\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n原文确实提到 English language lessons，但那是说有了车之后通勤去上英语课的时间从三\n小时减到一小时，机构并没有为 Leila 付英语课学费。B 项 It paid for her English lessons.\n把听到的 English lessons 原词搬过来，却安了个原文没有的 paid for，是典型「听到原词\n就想选」的陷阱。\n借用原文：they used to spend walking to and from English language lessons\nC) 原词陷阱\n原文里 Leila 起初是自己 walk to the supermarket for basic supplies（走去超市买生活必\n需品），这是说她从前的困境，并非机构帮她送货。C 项 It delivered her daily\nnecessities. 借用了 supplies/necessities 这层「生活必需品」的原词印象，编出一个机构\n「配送」的动作，实际机构给的是车不是货。\n借用原文：walk to the supermarket for basic supplies\nD) 过度推断\n原文说 Leila 骑车后 her depression had begun to ease（抑郁开始缓解），这是骑车带\n来的心理变化，并不等于机构提供了 physical therapy（理疗）。D 项 It provided her\nwith physical therapy. 抓住「身体/康复」的联想做了过度推断，原文从没说过理疗。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What is ReBicycle doing to help those in need?", "options": ["A) Expanding bike-riding lessons.", "B) Providing free public transport.", "C) Offering walking tours to visitors.", "D) Asking local people for donations."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Expanding bike-riding lessons.\n【定位】To date, ReBicycle has donated more than 200 bikes to those in need, and is now expanding\nbike-riding lessons as demand soars.\n迄今为止，ReBicycle 已向有需要的人捐赠了 200 多辆自行车，如今正随着需求激增而扩大\n自行车骑行课程。\n【信号】now + 进行时标出当下举措。句中 is now expanding bike-riding lessons 同时含 now\n和进行时 is expanding，正是「当下正在做的举措」的信号；题干 those in need 也原样\n出现在这句，双重提示这就是答案句。\n【替换】is now expanding bike-riding lessons ↔ Expanding bike-riding lessons.\n几乎原句压缩，去掉时态助词\n【排除】B) 偷换概念\n原文讲的是给人自行车、让人能 travel farther but for almost no cost（几乎零成本走得\n更远），核心是「自行车」。B 项 Providing free public transport. 借「免费、出行」的\n听感，把自行车偷换成 public transport（公共交通），概念被掉包，原文从未说提供公共\n交通。\n借用原文：travel farther but for almost no cost\nC) 原词陷阱\n原文提到人们从前 they used to spend walking（过去常常靠走路）通勤，是要被自行车\n取代的旧状态。C 项 Offering walking tours to visitors. 抓住 walking 这个原词，编出\n「为游客提供步行游」，既反了原文（机构是要减少走路、不是组织走路），也无中生有了\nvisitors。\n借用原文：The three hours a day, they used to spend walking to and from English language lessons\nD) 偷换概念\n原文说机构 urgently looking for more volunteers（急招志愿者），求的是人手不是钱。\nD 项 Asking local people for donations. 把「求助于本地人」这层意思保留，却把要的东\n西换成 donations（捐款），张冠李戴成募捐，原文没有向人要钱。\n借用原文：we are urgently looking for more volunteers", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What do we learn from the passage about ReBicycle?", "options": ["A) It is a sports club.", "B) It is a language school.", "C) It is a counseling center.", "D) It is a charity organization."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （主旨题） It is a charity organization.\n【定位】Today, I would like to talk to you about my charity ReBicycle. ... To date, ReBicycle has\ndonated more than 200 bikes to those in need ... we are urgently looking for more volunteers.\n今天我想跟大家谈谈我的慈善机构 ReBicycle。……迄今为止，ReBicycle 已向有需要的人捐\n赠了 200 多辆自行车……我们正急切地寻找更多志愿者。\n【信号】开头自报性质 + 全篇行为印证。开篇 my charity ReBicycle 直接点明它是 charity（慈善机\n构），再由 has donated more than 200 bikes to those in need 免费捐车、looking for\nmore volunteers 招募志愿者等行为印证——开头点题加全篇行为合起来锁定性质，是主旨\n题的定位信号。\n【替换】my charity ReBicycle ↔ a charity organization\ncharity → charity organization 概括化\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n原文提到 Leila 曾被邀请加入 a local bike club（当地一个自行车俱乐部），那是另一个组\n织，不是 ReBicycle 本身。A 项 It is a sports club. 借 bike club 的「俱乐部/运动」印象，\n把 ReBicycle 错认成体育俱乐部，张冠李戴到了别的组织头上。\n借用原文：she was invited to join a local bike club\nB) 原词陷阱\n原文里 English language lessons 只是用来说明有了车之后通勤时间缩短，机构本身不教\n英语。B 项 It is a language school. 抓 English lessons 的原词，把机构错定成语言学校，\n原文从未说它是教语言的学校。\n借用原文：walking to and from English language lessons\nC) 过度推断\n原文说 Leila 起初 quite down and unhappy、骑车后 depression 缓解，这是受助者的情\n绪变化。C 项 It is a counseling center. 抓住「情绪低落、好转」的线索，过度推断机构是\n心理咨询中心，原文从没说它做心理咨询。\n借用原文：After a few months of being quite down and unhappy", "points": ["听力·讲座", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What do NASA scientists want to learn about?", "options": ["A) How animals deal with lack of gravity.", "B) How mice interact in a new environment.", "C) How low gravity affects the human body.", "D) How mice imitate human behavior in space."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） How animals deal with lack of gravity.\n【定位】To that end, they have been studying how other species deal with low gravity, specifically\nfocusing on mice.\n为此，他们一直在研究其他物种如何应对低重力，尤其聚焦于老鼠。\n【信号】目的信号 To that end 引出研究指向。To that end（为此）是承接上文'想了解更多'的目的\n信号，其后 studying how other species deal with low gravity 正是科学家想了解的内\n容，是承载答案的那一句。\n【替换】how other species deal with low gravity ↔ How animals deal with lack of gravity.\nother species=animals、low gravity=lack of gravity 的同义替换\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nHow mice interact in a new environment. 借用了原文反复出现的 mice 来诱选，但研究\n内容是'应对低重力'，不是 interact（互相往来）；mice 是真的，interact 是无中生有。\n借用原文：specifically focusing on mice\nC) 原词陷阱\nHow low gravity affects the human body. 盗用了开头的 the effects of low gravity on\nthe human body 原词，但原文说人体方面'we know quite a bit'（已知不少），科学\n家'want to learn more'转去研究老鼠，故对人体的研究是旧信息，不是本题问的'想了解什\n么'。\n借用原文：we know quite a bit about the effects of low gravity on the human body\nD) 未提及\nHow mice imitate human behavior in space. 拼接了 mice 和太空场景的原词，但原文从\n未说老鼠 imitate human behavior（模仿人类行为），是凭空捏造的研究内容。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What does the passage say about the mice at the beginning of the experiment?", "options": ["A) They found the space in the cage too small to stay in.", "B) They found it difficult to figure out where they were.", "C) They were not used to the low-gravity environment.", "D) They were not sensitive to the changed environment."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They were not used to the low-gravity environment.\n【定位】As you'll notice in the video, the mice definitely seem uncomfortable at the beginning of the\nexperiment. They move around clumsily, drifting within the small confines of the cage, and\ndo their best to figure out which way is up, but without success.\n正如你在视频里会看到的，实验开始时老鼠明显显得不适应。它们笨拙地四处移动，在笼子\n狭小的空间里飘来飘去，竭力想搞清哪边是上，却没成功。\n【信号】时间锚 at the beginning + 状态词 uncomfortable。at the beginning of the\nexperiment 与题干时间锚对应，紧跟的 the mice definitely seem uncomfortable 点明\n开头状态是'不适应'，后句 do their best to figure out which way is up, but without\nsuccess 进一步具体化，是答案句。\n【替换】the mice definitely seem uncomfortable ↔ They were not used to the low-gravity environment.\nuncomfortable=not used to 的同义替换\ndo their best to figure out which way is up, but without success ↔\nnot used to the low-gravity environment\n辨不清上下=不适应失重的因果归纳\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nThey found the space in the cage too small to stay in. 借了原文 within the small\nconfines of the cage 里的 small 和 cage 原词，但 small confines 只是描述笼子空间小这\n个客观背景，原文从没说老鼠因此'待不下去（too small to stay in）'，是把背景词偷换成\n因果结论。\n借用原文：drifting within the small confines of the cage\nB) 偷换概念\nThey found it difficult to figure out where they were. 盗用原文 figure out which way\nis up 的 figure out，但原文说的是'辨不清哪边是上（方向/上下）'，不是'搞不清自己身在\n何处（where they were，位置）'，把'辨方向'偷换成了'辨位置'。\n借用原文：do their best to figure out which way is up\nD) 与原文相反\nThey were not sensitive to the changed environment. 说老鼠对环境变化不敏感，可原\n文明说它们 seem uncomfortable、笨拙地辨不清方向，恰恰是对变化高度有反应、很不适\n应，选项把极性说反了。\n借用原文：the mice definitely seem uncomfortable", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What was observed about the mice on the eleventh day of the experiment?", "options": ["A) They continued to behave as they did in the beginning.", "B) They already felt at home in the new environment.", "C) They had found a lot more activities to engage in.", "D) They tried everything possible to escape from the cage."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） They already felt at home in the new environment.\n【定位】The eleventh day of the experiment shows the mice are not just dealing with the gravity\nchange but actually seem to be enjoying it. Several of the mice are observed running around\nthe cage walls.\n实验第十一天显示，老鼠不只是在应对重力变化，实际上似乎还乐在其中。有几只老鼠被观\n察到绕着笼壁奔跑。\n【信号】时间锚 The eleventh day + 递进 not just…but actually。The eleventh day of the\nexperiment 与题干时间锚对齐，递进结构 not just dealing with the gravity change but\nactually seem to be enjoying it 把状态推到'乐在其中'，正是那天观察到的核心情形，是\n答案句。\n【替换】actually seem to be enjoying it ↔ They already felt at home in the new environment.\nenjoying it=felt at home 的同义替换\nnot just dealing with the gravity change ↔ already felt at home\n递进'不止应对'=已超越适应、到自在\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nThey continued to behave as they did in the beginning. 说还跟开头一样，但原文第十\n一天明说它们从 uncomfortable 变到 enjoying it，状态已大变，A 与原文的变化方向相\n反。它还会蹭第25题'保持作息'的印象诱你混选。\n借用原文：the mice definitely seem uncomfortable at the beginning\nC) 过度推断\nThey had found a lot more activities to engage in. 借原文 running around the cage\nwalls 让人觉得'活动变多'，但原文只说有几只绕笼壁跑，从没说它们'找到了更多种活动（a\nlot more activities）'，是把一个动作夸大成'活动种类增多'的过度推断。\n借用原文：running around the cage walls\nD) 未提及\nThey tried everything possible to escape from the cage. 盗用原文反复出现的 cage，\n且第23题选项里也埋过'逃笼'的诱饵，但原文从头到尾没说老鼠想 escape（逃跑），它们是\n适应并享受，不是想逃。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What did the scientists find about the mice from the experiment?", "options": ["A) They changed their routines in space.", "B) They began to eat less after some time.", "C) They behaved as if they were on Earth.", "D) They repeated their activities every day."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They behaved as if they were on Earth.\n【定位】The scientists wanted to see whether the mice would continue doing the same kinds of\nactivities they were observed doing on Earth. The study showed that the mice kept much of\ntheir routines intact, including cleaning themselves and eating when hungry.\n科学家想看看老鼠是否会继续做它们在地球上被观察到的那类活动。研究显示，老鼠的大部\n分日常作息保持不变，包括给自己清洁和饿了就吃。\n【信号】结论信号 The study showed 引出末句发现。The study showed 是篇章结尾引出结论的\n信号，其后 the mice kept much of their routines intact 正是实验的总结性发现，是答案\n句；前句的 on Earth 提供了对照参照。\n【替换】the mice kept much of their routines intact ↔ They behaved as if they were on Earth.\n保持地球上的作息=表现得像在地球\ncleaning themselves and eating when hungry ↔ behaved as if they were on Earth\n举例的地球式日常→概括为像在地球\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nThey changed their routines in space. 盗用原文 routines 和太空场景，但原文明说 kept\nmuch of their routines intact（保持作息不变），A 说 changed（改变了），极性相反。\n借用原文：the mice kept much of their routines intact\nB) 原词陷阱\nThey began to eat less after some time. 盗用原文 eating when hungry 的 eat 原词，\n但原文只说'饿了就吃'（作息照旧的例子之一），从没说'吃得变少（eat less）'，是把'照常\n吃'偷换成'吃得少'。\n借用原文：eating when hungry\nD) 偷换概念\nThey repeated their activities every day. 借'保持日常活动'的意思，但加了 every day\n（每天）这一原文没有的绝对化限定，原文只说 kept much of their routines（保持大部分\n作息），并未说'每天重复'，属把范围/频率说死。\n借用原文：the mice kept much of their routines intact", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) constantly", "B) credible", "C) essential", "D) exploring", "E) gather", "F) load", "G) miserable", "H) pressure", "I) properly", "J) records", "K) removed", "L) stacks", "M) suspicion", "N) tracked", "O) watching"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) essential\n形容词\n【词性槽】系动词 is 后作表语，要形容词\n【依据】呼应首句「信任是根本」，trust is essential＝信任不可或缺\n第一段已定调 Trust is fundamental to life.，第二段 too 表示职场同理，所以这里说信任\n「至关重要」最顺，essential 正是「必不可少」之意。\n【竞争词】B) credible 语义错位：可信的对象是人/说法，不是「信任」本身\ncredible 意为「可信的」，说 trust is credible（信任是可信的）逻辑打架；句子要表达的是信\n任的重要性，不是信任可不可信。\nG) miserable 感情色彩反：信任本身不会「悲惨」\nmiserable 是负面词「悲惨的」，本句在肯定信任的价值，色彩相反；它真正的归宿是第 28 空\nyou'll have a __(28)__ time。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) constantly", "B) credible", "C) essential", "D) exploring", "E) gather", "F) load", "G) miserable", "H) pressure", "I) properly", "J) records", "K) removed", "L) stacks", "M) suspicion", "N) tracked", "O) watching"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) suspicion\n名词\n【词性槽】and 并列 fear，要与之同为名词\n【依据】「缺乏信任」最直接的结果就是猜疑\nAn organization without trust 没有信任，自然 full of fear and suspicion（充满恐惧与\n猜疑），suspicion 与 trust 正是反义对子，语义最贴。\n【竞争词】H) pressure 义偏：压力非「不信任」的直接对应，且另有专属空\npressure 指「压力」，与「没有信任」不构成直接因果；它真正对应第 35 空 How much of\nour work __(35)__ would disappear? 的 work pressure（工作压力）。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) constantly", "B) credible", "C) essential", "D) exploring", "E) gather", "F) load", "G) miserable", "H) pressure", "I) properly", "J) records", "K) removed", "L) stacks", "M) suspicion", "N) tracked", "O) watching"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) miserable\n形容词\n【词性槽】在名词 time 前作定语，要形容词\n【依据】在不信任你的老板手下，日子很难受\n前提是 If you work for a boss who doesn't trust their employees，被处处盯防、纠错、\n催促，have a miserable time（日子很难受）色彩与情境吻合。\n【竞争词】B) credible 搭配＋色彩双错：credible time 不成立\ncredible「可信的」修饰 time 讲不通（没有「可信的时光」一说），且本句是负面情境，需要\n贬义词，credible 中性偏褒，色彩也不对。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) constantly", "B) credible", "C) essential", "D) exploring", "E) gather", "F) load", "G) miserable", "H) pressure", "I) properly", "J) records", "K) removed", "L) stacks", "M) suspicion", "N) tracked", "O) watching"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) constantly\n副词\n【词性槽】修饰现在分词 reminding，要副词\n【依据】呼应「all the time」，描述不停地提醒\n前句 They'll be checking up on you all the time 已说「一直盯着」，本句 constantly\n（不断地）reminding 与之同调，刻画不信任的老板没完没了地提醒。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) constantly", "B) credible", "C) essential", "D) exploring", "E) gather", "F) load", "G) miserable", "H) pressure", "I) properly", "J) records", "K) removed", "L) stacks", "M) suspicion", "N) tracked", "O) watching"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) watching\n动词\n【词性槽】spend time doing 结构，空填动名词\n【依据】watch one's back＝提防背后、互相防备\n上下文是互不信任的同事 Colleagues who don't trust one another，watching their\nbacks（时刻提防）正刻画这种防备，比埋头干活还费时间，语义贴切。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) constantly", "B) credible", "C) essential", "D) exploring", "E) gather", "F) load", "G) miserable", "H) pressure", "I) properly", "J) records", "K) removed", "L) stacks", "M) suspicion", "N) tracked", "O) watching"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) records\n名词\n【词性槽】动词 keep 的宾语，且被 large volumes of 修饰，要名词\n【依据】因不信任供应商客户而留存大量「记录/档案」\n下文给出原因 because they don't trust their suppliers, their contractors or their\ncustomers——不信任就要留底，keep large volumes of records（保存大量记录）逻辑\n最顺。\n【竞争词】F) load 义不合：load 是「负荷」，公司不会「保存负荷」\nload 指负担/负荷，keep large volumes of load 讲不通；它对应第 33 空的动词用法，不在\n此处。\nL) stacks 搭配冲突：stacks 本身已含「大量」之意，与 volumes of 语义重复\nstacks 意为「一堆、大量」，large volumes of stacks（大量的『一大堆』）语义叠床架屋，且\n不指向「因不信任而留底」的具体对象。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 L) —— stacks 词性也对、又含『大量』，最易误选。stacks 是名\n词且自带「一堆」之意，填进 large volumes of __(31)__ 看着顺；防坑：volumes of 已\n经表大量，stacks 与之语义重复，且要选「因不信任而留存的东西」——记录(records)才\n扣题。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) constantly", "B) credible", "C) essential", "D) exploring", "E) gather", "F) load", "G) miserable", "H) pressure", "I) properly", "J) records", "K) removed", "L) stacks", "M) suspicion", "N) tracked", "O) watching"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) removed\n动词\n【词性槽】could be ___ 被动语态，空填过去分词\n【依据】无价值的工作被「去掉」，才能省下数百万\n下文 the savings would run into millions of dollars——只有把 valueless work 去除\n（removed）才能省钱，removed（被去除）与「省钱」因果直连。\n【竞争词】N) tracked 逻辑反：追踪工作不会带来节省\ntracked「被追踪」与下文「省下数百万」对不上——追踪只会增加工作而非减少；本句要的是\n「去掉」这类减负动作。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 N) —— tracked 形态对、又像审计『追踪』，迷惑性强。tracked\n能构成 could be tracked 的被动，且全文提到 audit，易被带偏；防坑：认准结果句 the\nsavings would run into millions，省钱只能靠『去除』(removed)，追踪是增负，方向\n相反。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) constantly", "B) credible", "C) essential", "D) exploring", "E) gather", "F) load", "G) miserable", "H) pressure", "I) properly", "J) records", "K) removed", "L) stacks", "M) suspicion", "N) tracked", "O) watching"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) load\n动词\n【词性槽】we ___ onto ourselves，主语 we 后缺谓语，要动词\n【依据】load ... onto＝把（负担）加到……身上\n本段说这些都是因不信任而 extra work we __(33)__ onto ourselves，load onto oneself\n（把额外活儿压到自己头上）是 load 作动词的典型搭配，语义贴切。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) constantly", "B) credible", "C) essential", "D) exploring", "E) gather", "F) load", "G) miserable", "H) pressure", "I) properly", "J) records", "K) removed", "L) stacks", "M) suspicion", "N) tracked", "O) watching"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) properly\n副词\n【词性槽】修饰动词 do them，要副词\n【依据】与后文「or at all」对照，意为「把事做对/做好」\ndo them __(34)__—or at all 中 or at all 表「甚至根本不做」，前面应是「好好地做」，\nproperly（妥当地、正确地）正好构成「做不好——甚至不做」的递进。\n【竞争词】A) constantly 义不搭：此处讲「做得好不好」，不是「做得勤不勤」\nconstantly 意为「不断地」，与 or at all（根本不做）形成不了对照；本空要的是质量评价，不\n是频率，且 constantly 另有归宿（第 29 空）。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) constantly", "B) credible", "C) essential", "D) exploring", "E) gather", "F) load", "G) miserable", "H) pressure", "I) properly", "J) records", "K) removed", "L) stacks", "M) suspicion", "N) tracked", "O) watching"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) pressure\n名词\n【词性槽】名词 work 后构成复合名词，作主语中心，要名词\n【依据】work pressure＝工作压力，固定常见搭配\n本段说去掉不信任带来的额外活儿后会怎样：How much of our work __(35)__ would\ndisappear?，work pressure（工作压力）是地道搭配，且「压力消失」逻辑顺。\n【竞争词】F) load 搭配略逊且本词另有更贴的空\nload 也可作名词，但 work pressure 比 work load 在此更顺，且 load 真正对应第 33 空的动\n词用法 extra work we __(33)__ onto ourselves，把它留给那里更合理。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "According to some people living in San Jose, it has become much harder for the poor to get ahead due to the increased inequality.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】穷人变难 + 不平等加剧\n【定位】Some San Jose residents say that as inequality has grown in recent years, upward mobility has\nbecome much more difficult to achieve.\n一些圣何塞居民表示，随着近些年不平等加剧，向上流动变得困难得多。\n【改写】According to some people living in San Jose ↔ Some San Jose residents say\n同源引语者改写\ndue to the increased inequality ↔ as inequality has grown in recent years\n状语从句到名词短语的等值改写\nmuch harder for the poor to get ahead ↔\nupward mobility has become much more difficult to achieve\n动词短语 vs 名词化的同义改写\n【辨邻】 G G 段表述为 Whether the city still allows for upward mobility of poor kids today,\nthough, is up for debate，只是把今天能否上升流动作为 \"有争议\" 来呈现，并没有以\n居民为发声主体说 \"因为不平等加剧所以穷人难上升\"，因此不是 36 题的源头段。\nWhether the city still allows for upward mobility of poor kids today, though, is up for debate. Some\nof the indicators such as income inequality, measured by the Equality of Opportunity Project for the\nyear 2000, have only worsened in the past 16 years.\nF F 段提到 one of the most unequal places 与 racial and economic segregation，\n但语境是研究者承认 \"按这些指标本不该流动性高，可它当年偏偏高\"——是讲历史反\n常之谜，不是讲 \"今天因不平等加剧所以难上升\"，故非源头段。\nIt is one of the most unequal places out of the 741 that the researchers measured, and it has high\ndegrees of racial and economic segregation", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "In American history, immigrants used to have a good chance to move upward in society.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】历史上移民有良好上升机会\n【定位】This is a city of immigrants—38 percent of the city's population today is foreign-born—\nand immigrants and their children have historically experienced significant upward mobility\nin America.\n这是一座移民之城——如今 38% 的人口是外国出生的——而移民及其子女在历史上一直在\n美国经历着显著的向上流动。\n【改写】In American history ↔ historically experienced significant upward mobility in America\n时间副词与介词短语等值\nimmigrants used to have a good chance to move upward in society ↔\nimmigrants and their children have historically experienced significant upward mobility\n主语扩展与动名化等值\n【辨邻】 A A 段以个体故事 Tri Tran 引入主题，重在叙事，未给出 \"移民群体在美国历史上向上流\n动\" 这一概括论断。故不是 37 题源头段。\nTri Tran, who fled Vietnam on a boat in 1986, showed up in San Jose with nothing, made it to MIT,\nand then founded the food-delivery start-up Munchery, which is valued at $300 million.\nE E 段罗列 Vietnamese immigrants, Mexican immigrants, Korean immigrants, and\nFilipino immigrants 等族裔小生意的现状，呈现的是 \"今天街景\"，并未做 \"移民在美\n国历史上有上升机会\" 的论断，所以不是 37 题源头段。\nthere are areas filled with small businesses owned by Vietnamese immigrants, Mexican immigrants,\nKorean immigrants, and Filipino immigrants, to name a few", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "If the problems of San Jose can't be solved, one of America's fundamental beliefs about itself can be shaken.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】问题解不开 + 动摇美国根本信念\n【定位】That such mobility has become more difficult in San Jose raises questions about the\nendurance of that foundational belief. After all, if the one-time land of opportunity can't be\nfixed, what does that say for the rest of America?\n向上流动在圣何塞变得更困难，这件事引发了对那一根本信念能否持续的质疑。毕竟，如果\n这片曾经的机会之地都修复不了，那对美国其他地方又意味着什么？\n【改写】If the problems of San Jose can't be solved ↔ if the one-time land of opportunity can't be fixed\n上下位概念的指称等值\none of America's fundamental beliefs about itself can be shaken ↔\nraises questions about the endurance of that foundational belief\n同义形容词与因果方向相同\n【辨邻】 J J 段反问 can the San Jose region really once again become a place of\nopportunity? 只针对圣何塞这一城市层级的疑问，没说 \"美国关于自身的根本信念被\n动摇\"，故不是 38 题源头段。\nBut in today's America—a land of rising inequality, increasing segregation, and stagnating middle-\nclass wages—can the San Jose region really once again become a place of opportunity?", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "San Jose was among the best cities in America for poor kids to move up the social ladder.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】圣何塞在全美数一数二利于穷孩子向上\n【定位】Kids in San Jose whose families fell in the bottom quintile of income nationally had the best\nshot in the country at reaching the top quintile.\n在圣何塞，家庭收入全国处于最底层五分位的孩子，在全国范围内拥有进入最顶层五分位的\n最佳机会。\n【改写】San Jose was among the best cities in America ↔\nhad the best shot in the country at reaching the top quintile\n全国最佳=全美最好之一\nfor poor kids to move up the social ladder ↔\nKids in San Jose whose families fell in the bottom quintile of income nationally had the best\nshot...at reaching the top quintile\n隐喻与数据指标等值\n【辨邻】 C C 段是 just 4.4 percent of poor kids in Charlotte moved up to the top; in Detroit\nthe figure was 5.5 percent 这种与他城对比的论述（这恰好是 43 题的源头），但 39\n题问的是 \"在全美最好之一\" 的绝对定位，需要 B 段中 the best shot in the country\n这种 \"全国\" 字样，不是 C 段的横比。\nBy contrast, just 4.4 percent of poor kids in Charlotte moved up to the top; in Detroit the figure was", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Whether poor kids in San Jose today still have the chance to move upward is questionable.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】今日穷孩子是否还能上升 = 有疑问\n【定位】Whether the city still allows for upward mobility of poor kids today, though, is up for debate.\n不过，这座城市今天是否仍然允许穷孩子向上流动，是有待商榷的。\n【改写】Whether poor kids in San Jose today still have the chance to move upward ↔\nWhether the city still allows for upward mobility of poor kids today\n主从语态互换、信息完全对齐\nis questionable ↔ is up for debate\n习语与单词的同义\n【辨邻】 H H 段最后明确判断 the future for the region's poor doesn't look nearly as bright\nas it once did——这已经是确定性结论，不是 \"is questionable / up for debate\" 的\n疑问语气；故不是 40 题源头段。\nGiven this, the future for the region's poor doesn't look nearly as bright as it once did.\nJ J 段 can the San Jose region really once again become a place of opportunity?\n反问的主语是 \"圣何塞地区\" + \"再次成为机会之地\"，与 40 题 \"穷孩子今天是否还有上\n升机会\" 的主语和谓语都不完全对齐；G 段才是直接对应的陈述存疑。\ncan the San Jose region really once again become a place of opportunity?", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "San Jose's officials are resolved to give poor kids access to the resources necessary for success in life.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】官员决心给穷孩子提供资源\n【定位】Leaders in San Jose are determined to make sure that the city regains its status as a place\nwhere even poor kids can access the resources to succeed.\n圣何塞的领导者们决心确保这座城市重新成为连穷孩子都能获取成功所需资源的地方。\n【改写】San Jose's officials are resolved ↔ Leaders in San Jose are determined\n同义名词+同义形容词\nto give poor kids access to the resources necessary for success in life ↔\nplace where even poor kids can access the resources to succeed\n施动方向互换+不定式同义\n【辨邻】 J J 段 can the San Jose region really once again become a place of opportunity?\n是质疑性的反问，主语和发声者并非 \"官员/领导\"，态度也不是 \"决心\"，故不是 41 题\n源头段。\ncan the San Jose region really once again become a place of opportunity?", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "San Jose appears to manifest some of the best features of America.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】体现美国最好的一面\n【定位】Indeed, the streets of San Jose seem, in some ways, to embody the best of America.\n确实，从某种程度上说，圣何塞的街道似乎体现了美国最好的一面。\n【改写】San Jose appears to manifest some of the best features of America ↔\nthe streets of San Jose seem, in some ways, to embody the best of America\n整句结构与选词逐对等值\n【辨邻】 A A 段开篇 This is the land of opportunity 只是给圣何塞贴 \"机会之地\" 的标签，并没\n有像 E 段那样把它概括为 \"体现美国最好一面\"。故 A 段不是 42 题源头段。\nThis is the land of opportunity.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "As far as social mobility is concerned, San Jose beat many other progressive cities in America.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】圣何塞胜过其他进步城市\n【定位】San Jose had social mobility comparable to Denmark's and Canada's and higher than other\nprogressive cities such as Boston and Minneapolis.\n圣何塞的社会流动性可与丹麦、加拿大相提并论，并且高于波士顿、明尼阿波利斯等其他进\n步城市。\n【改写】As far as social mobility is concerned ↔ San Jose had social mobility\n话题界定的等值\nSan Jose beat many other progressive cities in America ↔\nhigher than other progressive cities such as Boston and Minneapolis\n比较动词与比较结构的同义\n【辨邻】 B B 段 the best shot in the country at reaching the top quintile 只给出 \"全美最\n高\" 的定位，没有把波士顿、明尼阿波利斯等其他进步城市拿来比较，故不是 43 题源\n头段。\nKids in San Jose whose families fell in the bottom quintile of income nationally had the best shot in\nthe country at reaching the top quintile.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Due to some changes like increases in housing prices in San Jose, the prospects for its poor people have dimmed.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】房价上涨等变化 → 穷人前景黯淡\n【定位】San Jose used to have a happy mix of factors—cheap housing, closeness to a rapidly\ndeveloping industry, tightly-knit immigrant communities—that together opened up the\npossibility of prosperity for even its poorest residents. But in recent years, housing prices\nhave skyrocketed, the region's rich and poor have segregated, and middle-class jobs have\ndisappeared. Given this, the future for the region's poor doesn't look nearly as bright as it\nonce did.\n圣何塞曾经有一套很合拍的有利因素——便宜的住房、毗邻高速发展的产业、紧密团结的\n移民社区——这些因素一起为最贫穷的居民也打开了通向繁荣的可能。但近年来，房价飙\n升、贫富分化加剧、中产工作消失。鉴于此，这一地区穷人的未来已远不如从前看上去那么\n光明。\n【改写】Due to some changes like increases in housing prices in San Jose ↔\nBut in recent years, housing prices have skyrocketed\n因果连词与时间副词的等值\nthe prospects for its poor people have dimmed ↔\nthe future for the region's poor doesn't look nearly as bright as it once did\n光暗隐喻的同义\n【辨邻】 G G 段表述为 Some of the indicators such as income inequality, measured by the\nEquality of Opportunity Project for the year 2000, have only worsened in the\npast 16 years，谈的是收入不平等等指标，并未把 \"房价上涨\" 作为具体因素列出，故\n不是 44 题源头段。\nSome of the indicators such as income inequality, measured by the Equality of Opportunity Project\nfor the year 2000, have only worsened in the past 16 years.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Researchers do not have a clear idea why poor children in San Jose achieved such great success several decades ago.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】研究者说不清几十年前穷孩子为何成功\n【定位】But researchers aren't sure exactly why poor kids in San Jose did so well.\n但研究者并不能确切说明，圣何塞的穷孩子为什么当年表现这么好。\n【改写】Researchers do not have a clear idea ↔ researchers aren't sure exactly why\n同义否定表达\npoor children in San Jose achieved such great success several decades ago ↔\npoor kids in San Jose did so well\n结果论断同义+时间隐含等值\n【辨邻】 B B 段 the best shot in the country at reaching the top quintile 是给出 \"穷孩子表\n现好\" 的数据结果，并未承认 \"研究者不确定为什么\" 这一认知缺口，故不是 45 题源\n头段。\nA child born in the early 1980s into a low-income family in San Jose had a 12.9 percent chance of\nbecoming a high earner as an adult\nD D 段 The reasons kids in San Jose performed so well might seem obvious 之后\n罗列了创新公司、移民比例、多元化等具体原因解释，是在给原因；而 45 题要的是\n\"研究者不清楚原因\"，论旨方向相反，故不是源头段。\nThe reasons kids in San Jose performed so well might seem obvious.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What are teachers complaining about?", "options": ["A) There are too many students requiring special attention.", "B) They are under too much stress counselling needy students.", "C) Schools are inadequately equipped to implement any intervention.", "D) They lack the necessary resources to address pupils' mental problems."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What are teachers complaining about? 用 what 直接问一个客观事实，没有\ninfer/suggest/imply 之类的推断信号词。\n【拆句】题干 What are teachers complaining about? 是现在进行时的特殊疑问句，主语\nteachers、谓语 are complaining about，介词 about 后被前置疑问词 what 取代，无从\n句、无否定，结构平铺直叙、无陷阱。\n【定位】But teachers also consistently report how ill-equipped they feel to meet pupils' mental health\nneeds, and often cite a lack of training, expertise and support from the National Health\nService(英国国家医疗服务体系).\n但是教师们也一贯反映自己在应对学生心理健康需求时装备多么不足，并经常列举培训、专\n业能力和来自英国国家医疗服务体系支持的缺乏。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nThere are too many students requiring special attention. 把抱怨内容偷换成学生数\n量过多，但原文 a lack of training, expertise and support 抱怨的是教师自身资源不\n足，并未说学生人数太多。\nB) 偷换概念\nThey are under too much stress counselling needy students. 把抱怨内容换成教师\n承受过大压力，但原文 how ill-equipped they feel 抱怨的是装备不足，不是工作压\n力大。\nC) 以偏概全\nSchools are inadequately equipped to implement any intervention. 用 any\nintervention（任何干预）把范围放大；原文只说教师在应对心理需求上装备不足，并\n未说学校不能实施任何干预。\nD) ✓ 正确\na lack of training, expertise and support ↔ lack the necessary resources\nto meet pupils' mental health needs ↔ to address pupils' mental problems\n预判的两个语义点（缺资源、为了应对学生心理问题）都精确落在 They lack the\nnecessary resources to address pupils' mental problems. 里，故锁定。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What do we learn from the passage about community health services in Britain?", "options": ["A) They have deteriorated due to budget cuts.", "B) They facilitate local residents' everyday lives.", "C) They prove ineffective in helping mental patients.", "D) They cover preventative care for the local resident."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn from the passage about community health services in Britain? 中\nlearn from the passage 是定向取信息，不是推断，问的是文中已陈述的事实。\n【拆句】题干结构为 What + do we learn + from the passage + about + community health\nservices in Britain；about 短语限定要问的对象，in Britain 进一步限定地域。理解时\n把 What do we learn 视作信息询问的固定外壳，重心放在 about 后面那串名词短语，无\n陷阱。\n【定位】Cuts to local authority budgets since 2010 have resulted in a significant decline of these\nservices, despite strong evidence of their effectiveness in preventing crises further down\nthe line.\n自 2010 年以来对地方财政预算的削减导致了这些服务的大幅衰退，尽管有强有力的证据证\n明它们在预防后续危机方面的有效性。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nCuts to local authority budgets since 2010 have resulted in a significant decline of these services\n↔ They have deteriorated due to budget cuts.\na significant decline of these services ↔ They have deteriorated\n预判的两个要点（衰退 + 预算削减为因）都精确落在 They have deteriorated due to\nbudget cuts.，且因果方向一致，故锁定。\nB) 无中生有\nThey facilitate local residents' everyday lives. 说社区服务便利居民日常生活，但原文\n从未谈到对日常生活的便利，只谈预算与衰退。\nC) 与原文相反\nThey prove ineffective in helping mental patients. 说服务被证明无效，但原文\nstrong evidence of their effectiveness in preventing crises further down the line\n明确说有强证据证明其有效——正反相反。\nD) 偷换概念\nThey cover preventative care for the local resident. 看似呼应 preventing crises，\n但 preventative care 通常指预防性医疗保健，与原文 preventing crises further\ndown the line（预防后续危机）不是一回事；且本题问的是文章对社区服务的描述要\n点，原文重点是衰退，不是预防覆盖。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "Where does the author suggest mental health services be placed?", "options": ["A) At home.", "B) At school.", "C) In hospitals.", "D) In communities."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 Where does the author suggest mental health services be placed? 用 where 直\n问地点，配 suggest 提示要找作者明确写出的建议位置，仍是文章里已有的事实陈述。\n【拆句】题干 Where does the author suggest mental health services be placed? 表层是特殊疑\n问句，深层是 suggest + that 从句的虚拟语气结构——从句省略了 that，谓语用 (should)\nbe placed 的原形被动，should 被省略。基础薄弱考生易把 be placed 误读成时态错误，\n其实是 suggest 后建议性虚拟。\n【定位】The only way to break the pressures on both mental health services and schools is to reinvest\nin early intervention services inside schools.\n打破心理健康服务和学校双方压力的唯一办法是把早期干预服务重新投入到学校之内。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nAt home. 在家中提供，原文 inside schools 与 Schools see young people more\nthan any other service 反复强调学校，从未提到在家中提供心理服务。\nB) ✓ 正确\ninside schools ↔ At school.\n预判要求的地点是学校内部，四个选项里只有 At school. 对应，故锁定。\nC) 与原文相反\nIn hospitals. 与原文 a clinical setting can sometimes be daunting 的态度相反——\n作者把医院/诊所环境视为令人却步的，反对在医院里提供低强度服务。\nD) 张冠李戴\nIn communities. 看似呼应 low-level mental health services based in the\ncommunity，但作者讲的是社区服务因预算削减已衰退（Cuts to local authority\nbudgets… have resulted in a significant decline），主张方向已转向学校内部，不是\n继续放在社区。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What do we learn from the recent studies?", "options": ["A) Students prefer to rely on peers to relieve stress and anxiety.", "B) Young people are keen on building meaningful relationships.", "C) Students are more comfortable seeking counselling in school.", "D) Young people benefit from various kinds of outdoor activities."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn from the recent studies? 与第 47 题同句型，问的是研究直接给出的\n结论，不需要跨句推断。\n【拆句】题干 What do we learn from the recent studies? 与第 47 题同构，只是把 the passage\n换成 the recent studies，结构无陷阱。\n【定位】Recent studies have shown that children and young people largely prefer to see a counsellor\nin school rather than in an outside environment.\n近期研究表明，儿童和年轻人在很大程度上更愿意在学校里见心理咨询师，而不愿在校外环\n境中见。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nStudents prefer to rely on peers to relieve stress and anxiety. 称学生倾向于靠同\n龄人缓解压力焦虑，但研究结论是关于咨询地点（校内 vs 校外），从未谈到同伴这一\n路径。\nB) 张冠李戴\nYoung people are keen on building meaningful relationships. 与 build\nmeaningful relationships with them over time 关键词重合，但原文中这句话的主语\n是 Schools/them（即提供方），不是青少年自己；且本题问 recent studies 的结论，\n而不是这句一般性描述。\nC) ✓ 正确\nprefer to see a counsellor in school ↔ more comfortable seeking counselling in school\nchildren and young people ↔ Students\n预判的两个要点（学生群体 + 校内更愿）都精确落在 Students are more comfortable\nseeking counselling in school.，故锁定。\nD) 无中生有\nYoung people benefit from various kinds of outdoor activities. 谈户外活动的益\n处，原文未出现 outdoor activities 这一概念。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What does the author mean by a cultural shift (Line 2, Para. 6)?", "options": ["A) Simplification of schools' academic curriculums.", "B) Parents' involvement in schools' policy-making.", "C) A change in teachers' attitudes to mental health.", "D) A change in the conception of what schools are."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （词义句意题）\n【判型】题干 What does the author mean by a cultural shift 用 What does … mean by … 这一\n固定句型直问引文含义，是典型的词义句意题。\n【拆句】题干 What does the author mean by a cultural shift 是特殊疑问句，主干为 What does\nthe author mean，介词短语 by a cultural shift 引出被询问的短语，注意 mean by 在此\n是固定搭配（凭 X 意指什么），后跟括号注明的行号定位。\n【定位】Politicians, policymakers, commissioners and school leaders must be brave enough to make\nthe leap towards reimagining schools as providers of health as well as education services.\n政界、决策者、采购者和校领导必须有足够的勇气，迈出那一步，把学校重新想象成既提供\n健康服务、也提供教育服务的场所。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nSimplification of schools' academic curriculums. 简化学术课程，原文 combine\nmental health and wellbeing provision with a strong academic curriculum 强调学\n术课程要保持坚实，不是简化。\nB) 无中生有\nParents' involvement in schools' policy-making. 家长参与学校决策——原文呼吁的\n参与者是 Politicians, policymakers, commissioners and school leaders，并不包含\n家长，且谈的不是决策参与而是学校角色重塑。\nC) 以偏概全\nA change in teachers' attitudes to mental health. 把转变的范围缩窄到教师态度，但\n原文 Politicians, policymakers, commissioners and school leaders 列出的转变主体\n不是教师，而是政界、决策者、采购者和校领导——主体错位、范围缩小。\nD) ✓ 正确\nreimagining schools as providers of health as well as education services ↔\nA change in the conception of what schools are.\n预判的核心（重新定义学校是什么）与 D 的措辞一对一对齐，且 D 的 conception 一词\n正好对应 cultural shift 中的 cultural（观念层面的）。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "词义句意题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "Why does the author ask us to imagine buying food in the movie theater?", "options": ["A) To illustrate people's peculiar shopping behavior.", "B) To illustrate the increasing variety of snacks there.", "C) To show how hard it can be to choose a drink there.", "D) To show how popular snacks are among movie fans."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （例证目的题）\n【判型】Why does the author ask us to imagine buying food问的是作者为什么设置这个例子，\n不是问例子本身说了什么，属于例证目的题。\n【拆句】Why does the author ask us to imagine buying food in the movie theater?是一个why\n引导的特殊疑问句，主干为Why does the author ask us，to imagine buying food是ask\n的宾语补足语，in the movie theater作地点状语修饰imagine。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】If you're wondering who would buy the medium soda, the answer is almost no one. In fact,\nthere's a good chance the marketing department purposely priced the medium soda as a\ndecoy, making you more likely to buy the large soda rather than the small.\n如果你在想谁会去买中杯可乐，答案是几乎没人。事实上，营销部门很可能是故意把中杯定\n价为诱饵，让你更倾向于买大杯而不是小杯。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthere's a good chance the marketing department purposely priced the medium soda as a decoy,\nmaking you more likely to buy the large soda rather than the small\n↔ people's peculiar shopping behavior\nPicture this: You're at a movie theater food stand ↔ To illustrate\n预判到答案是\"展示异常购物行为\"，选项里只有To illustrate people's peculiar\nshopping behavior同时覆盖illustrate（举例目的）和peculiar shopping behavior\n（异常购物行为），与预判完全吻合。\nB) 无中生有\nthe increasing variety of snacks说的是零食\"种类的增多\"，但原文从头到尾只谈大中小\n三种可乐的定价心理，根本没有讨论零食种类多寡。\nC) 偷换概念\nhow hard it can be to choose a drink说的是\"选饮料有多难\"。原文It's a tough\ndecision确实承认决定难，但这只是引出诱饵效应的铺垫，作者的真正意图不是讲选择\n之难，而是讲选择被操纵；选项把铺垫当成目的，是偷换。\nD) 无中生有\nhow popular snacks are among movie fans说的是\"零食在影迷中多受欢迎\"，原文完\n全没有谈零食的受欢迎程度，也没把读者定义为movie fans。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "例证目的题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "Why is the medium soda priced the way it is?", "options": ["A) To attract more customers to buy it.", "B) To show the price matches the amount.", "C) To ensure customers drink the right amount of soda.", "D) To make customers believe they are getting a bargain."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】Why is the medium soda priced the way it is问的是文中已明示的因果，是对原文具体信\n息的再现，属于细节题。\n【拆句】Why is the medium soda priced the way it is?主干为被动结构the medium soda is\npriced，the way it is是方式状语从句简写（=in the way that it is priced），整体问的\n是\"为何如此定价\"。考生需读懂被动语态，把priced还原成\"被定价\"。\n【定位】In fact, there's a good chance the marketing department purposely priced the medium soda as\na decoy, making you more likely to buy the large soda rather than the small.\n事实上，营销部门很可能是故意把中杯定价为诱饵，让你更倾向于买大杯而不是小杯。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nTo attract more customers to buy it说的是\"吸引更多顾客来买中杯\"，但If you're\nwondering who would buy the medium soda, the answer is almost no one明确告\n诉我们中杯定价的目的恰恰不是为了卖中杯本身。\nB) 无中生有\nTo show the price matches the amount说的是\"展示价格与分量相符\"，但原文从未声\n称中杯价格合理，反而暗示这种定价是营销操纵。\nC) 偷换概念\nTo ensure customers drink the right amount of soda说的是\"确保顾客喝合适的量\"，\n体现的是关怀消费者健康的善意动机；原文中营销部门的动机是purposely priced the\nmedium soda as a decoy，即操纵性的，与善意正相反。\nD) ✓ 正确\npurposely priced the medium soda as a decoy, making you more likely to buy the large soda\nrather than the small\n↔ To make customers believe they are getting a bargain\nMedium may be the perfect amount of soda for you, but the large is only a quarter more ↔\ngetting a bargain\n预判答案是\"让顾客觉得大杯划算/占便宜\"，make customers believe they are getting\na bargain里getting a bargain正是\"占便宜\"的标准说法，与预判精准匹配。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What do we learn from Dan Ariely's experiment?", "options": ["A) Lower-priced goods attract more customers.", "B) The Economist's promotional strategy works.", "C) The Economist's print edition turns out to sell the best.", "D) More readers choose the digital over the print edition."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn from Dan Ariely's experiment问的是实验本身揭示了什么客观事实，\n是对原文的转述，属细节题。\n【拆句】What do we learn from Dan Ariely's experiment?是what引导的特殊疑问句，主干为we\nlearn，from Dan Ariely's experiment为状语。结构直白，无陷阱。\n【定位】Ariely ran an experiment and found that when only the two \"real\" choices were offered, more\npeople chose the less-expensive digital subscription. But the addition of the bad option made\npeople much more likely to choose the more expensive print plus digital option.\nAriely做了一个实验发现：只提供两个\"真实\"选项时，更多人选了便宜的纯数字订阅。但加\n入那个糟糕的选项后，人们更倾向于选更贵的纸质加数字组合订阅。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nLower-priced goods attract more customers说的是\"低价商品吸引更多顾客\"。原文\n在加诱饵后的结果恰是\"更多人选了更贵的\"，并非低价更受欢迎；实验的核心是揭示诱\n饵效应，不是廉价致胜。\nB) ✓ 正确\nthe addition of the bad option made people much more likely to choose the more expensive print\nplus digital option\n↔ The Economist's promotional strategy works\n预判答案是\"实验证明诱饵策略有效\"，The Economist's promotional strategy works\n里works即\"奏效、起作用\"，与预判中\"有效\"匹配；且promotional strategy恰好概括了\nThe Economist的特殊定价做法。\nC) 与原文相反\nThe Economist's print edition turns out to sell the best说的是\"纯纸质版卖得最\n好\"，但原文No one in their right mind would buy the print subscription when\nyou could get digital as well for the same price明确指出几乎没人单买纸质版——它\n根本不是最好卖的，而是诱饵本身。\nD) 以偏概全\nMore readers choose the digital over the print edition说的是\"更多读者选数字版而\n非纸质版\"。这只在But之前的场景（只有两个真实选项时）成立，But之后the addition\nof the bad option made people much more likely to choose the more expensive\nprint plus digital option改变了这个结果——而题问的是从整个实验中学到了什么，应\n当看But之后的发现。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "For what purpose is \"the bad option\" (Line 7, Para. 3) added?", "options": ["A) To cater to the peculiar needs of some customers.", "B) To help customers to make more rational choices.", "C) To trap customers into buying the more pricey item.", "D) To provide customers with a greater variety of goods."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】For what purpose is \"the bad option\" added问的是文中明示的添加目的，是对原文信息\n的客观转述，归为细节题。\n【拆句】For what purpose is \"the bad option\" (Line 7, Para. 3) added?是被动疑问句，For what\npurpose是疑问短语前置，主语是the bad option，谓语是is added。括号内的Line 7,\nPara. 3是位置提示，不算原文成分。\n【定位】But the addition of the bad option made people much more likely to choose the more\nexpensive print plus digital option.\n但加入那个糟糕的选项使得人们更倾向于选择更贵的\"纸质加数字\"组合订阅。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nTo cater to the peculiar needs of some customers说的是\"迎合某些顾客的特殊需\n求\"，但原文中加入诱饵的目的是营销操纵，不是为了满足顾客的任何需求；原文里No\none in their right mind would buy the print subscription更直接说明没人需要这个\n选项。\nB) 与原文相反\nTo help customers to make more rational choices说的是\"帮助顾客作出更理性的选\n择\"，但原文加入诱饵恰恰让顾客作出了非理性的选择——选了更贵的而非更便宜的；选\n项把操纵动机说成助人为乐，方向反了。\nC) ✓ 正确\nmade people much more likely to choose the more expensive print plus digital option ↔\nTo trap customers into buying the more pricey item\nthe more expensive print plus digital option ↔ the more pricey item\n预判答案为\"使顾客买更贵的\"，To trap customers into buying the more pricey item\n里trap...into精准对应\"诱饵\"的负面操纵色彩，more pricey精准对应more expensive，\n与预判完全匹配。\nD) 偷换概念\nTo provide customers with a greater variety of goods说的是\"为顾客提供更多种类的\n商品\"。原文中加诱饵不是为了丰富选项种类，而是为了利用对比心理把顾客推向最贵的\n那个选项。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2020-12-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "How do we assess the value of a commodity, according to the passage?", "options": ["A) By considering its usefulness.", "B) By comparing it with other choices.", "C) By taking its quality into account.", "D) By examining its value equation."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】How do we assess the value of a commodity, according to the passage?末尾\naccording to the passage显式要求从文本中找答案，属细节题。\n【拆句】How do we assess the value of a commodity, according to the passage?主干是How\ndo we assess the value，of a commodity是修饰value的后置定语，according to the\npassage是插入语，表示信息来源。结构清晰。\n【定位】Value is almost never absolute; rather, we decide an object's value relative to our\nother choices.\n价值几乎从不是绝对的；而是说，我们是相对于自己拥有的其他选项来决定一个物品的\n价值。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nBy considering its usefulness说的是\"考虑其用处\"，但原文Value is almost never\nabsolute; rather, we decide an object's value relative to our other choices明确指出\n价值是相对的，从未提到用处这个独立维度。\nB) ✓ 正确\nwe decide an object's value relative to our other choices ↔\nBy comparing it with other choices\ndecide an object's value ↔ assess the value\n预判答案为\"相对于其他选项\"，By comparing it with other choices的\ncomparing...with正是\"相对\"的动作化表达，与预判完全匹配。\nC) 无中生有\nBy taking its quality into account说的是\"将其质量纳入考量\"，原文从头到尾未谈商品\n质量；这是常识维度而非原文维度。\nD) 偷换概念\nBy examining its value equation说的是\"通过检视其价值等式\"。原文If more options\nare introduced, the value equation changes里的value equation是对评估过程的命\n名，不是用来评估的工具；选项把名词搬来制造熟悉，但用错了角色。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
